\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Shiokawa says  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  Friday. \b0\par\par\uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 06:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market earlier on Friday to prevent an unwanted rise in the yen. \par \par \uc2  He added that Japan's economic fundamentals warranted a slightly weaker yen and that Tokyo was determined to combat speculative moves to push up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  just a while ago," Shiokawa told a news conference. He added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. \par \par \uc2  "Given the strength of the Japanese economy, I wouldn't be surprised to see a slightly weaker yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has been buying dollars and euros for yen on an almost daily basis since last week to halt the yen's rise, which Tokyo fears could harm the nation's fragile and export-dependent economy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was hovering near 119.30 yen in afternoon trade in Asia on Friday after repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 120.00 yen for the first time since a big drop in the aftermath of the September 11 air attacks in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "Given the (state of the) current economy, which is in a recessionary phase, and given the problem of prices, I have been thinking that a weaker yen would be favourable," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  Economic data released on Friday painted a grim picture of falling prices. \par \par \uc2  The nationwide core consumer price index, which excludes volatile fresh food prices, fell for a 23rd month in a row, dropping 0.9 percent in August from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if the dollar rose above 120 yen, Shiokawa said: "I don't know how the pace (of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements) will be. If there is speculative movement in the market, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa denied that he had a certain yen level in mind when deciding to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but expressed determination to act again as necessary. \par \par \uc2  "It is true that there are speculative movements around the time of (end-September) book-closing," he said. "As long as such movements exist, I would like to take action. \par \par \uc2  "With the terrorist incident, we have to take a cautious look at the global situation. In that sense, I am watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates nervously," he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, senior Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said the yen's movement was still excessive and did not reflect economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  That was even after Japan aggressively sold the yen on Thursday, with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and New York Federal Reserve also selling it on Japan's behalf. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi declined to comment on the stance of overseas authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but added that Japan had been keeping in contact with other nations every day. \par \par \uc2  JGB CAP STILL ON \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa reiterated that there was no change in the government's plan keep new government bond issuance below 30 trillion yen ($250.7 billion). \par \par \uc2  Speculation has been rife that the government would have to abandon that plan for the current fiscal year to next March, especially after Koizumi said he would be "bold and flexible" in forming economic policy, making decisions based on economic conditions. \par \par \uc2  Bond market players in Tokyo have been on red alert for any increased issuance of Japanese government bonds, which could have the effect of pushing up long-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "I have no doubt that prime minister is sticking with 30 trillion yen," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  By "bold", Koizumi meant that he would take bold action in changing the quality of the fiscal spending and not the scale of the spending, Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  The 30 trillion yen limit for the new government bond issuance is one of the key policy pledges for the Koizumi administration as a way to rein in Japan's mushrooming public debt, which is approaching 130 percent of gross domestic product - the worst among major industrialised nations. ($1=119.68 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s007vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
