\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-U.S. stocks bounce higher - bonds rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 1,203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 04:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 27 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks staged a late-day rebound on Thursday, while U.S. Treasuries rose for a third straight day, as investors weighed the allure of cheap stock prices against gnawing worries about global recession and possible war. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street's blue-chip shares swung higher, mimicking the modest gains eked out on European exchanges, but the high-tech sector remained weak, pummeled by the incessant drumbeat of corporate profit warnings. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty over what the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States and the potential for U.S. military action will mean for an economy already teetering on the brink of recession have kept investors on edge and made for choppy trading. \par \par \uc2  "This is the way it's going to be for the foreseeable future, in that there are so many unknown variables out there," said Peter Coolidge, managing director of equity trading at Brean Murray & Co., adding that U.S. stocks had reached a level where buyers were willing to step in. \par \par \uc2  "As quickly as it has snapped back it can either keep going or it can turn and head south again," Coolidge said. \par \par \uc2  Another sign of investor anxiety came as bond prices surged, plunging yields on some shorter-term issues to historic lows, after fresh data pointed to deepening economic troubles and investors bet the U.S. Federal Reserve would respond with more interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  The yen hit a fresh two-week low against the dollar and a new one-month trough vs. the euro in U.S. trading as Japanese authorities fought to stave off the yen strength they fear will damage prospects for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices marched higher, continuing a rebound begun on Wednesday that broke seven straight days of decline amid fears faltering global economic health would crimp demand for gasoline and other products. \par \par \uc2  TECHNOLOGY LEADS STOCK SELL-OFF \par \par \uc2  Blue-chip shares led the market's climb, as investors flocked to health-care and industrial stocks, betting they are best placed to weather further economic travails, and bolstered by oil-related shares. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average rose 114.03 points, or 1.33 percent, to 8,681.42, while the broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index rose 11.57 points, or 1.15 percent, to 1,018.61. \par \par \uc2  However, high-tech shares remained pinned in negative territory, buffeted amid a slew of profit warnings from the likes of network equipment maker Sonus Networks Inc. , which said late Wednesday that it would post a loss in the current quarter as it sales fell 25 percent below expectations. \par \par \uc2  Sonus shares fell $3.52, or 55 percent, to $2.88 on Nasdaq. \par \par \uc2  The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index ended down 3.33 points, or 0.23 percent, at 1,460.71, biting further into the rebound eked out earlier this week in the wake of last week's brutal sell-off. \par \par \uc2  "The evidence is mounting that the world changed on Sept. 11. and the (U.S.) economy went into a recession," said Hugh Johnson, chief investment officer at First Albany Corp. "The message of the markets continues to be that there is no recovery in the economy and profits in sight." \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks had staged a rebound on Monday and Tuesday after major market gauges fell roughly 15 percent and the Dow racked up its biggest weekly drop last week since the Depression in the 1930s. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets eked out slim gains, bolstered by strength in energy and health-care heavyweights, but softness in technology shares held back their advance. Traders largely shrugged off the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave interest rates steady, as expected. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 rose 1.48 percent to 1,094.11, and the narrower DJ Euro Stoxx 50 rose 1.37 percent to 3,198.78. \par \par \uc2  Asian stock markets gained ground, as strength in select blue-chip firms bolstered Japanese stocks. Tokyo's key Nikkei 225 average rose 0.57 percent to 9,696.53, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng index rose 2.44 percent to 9,600.79. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC OUTLOOK DARKENS \par \par \uc2  Worries about the economy grew after the number of Americans signing up for U.S. jobless benefits hit their highest level in more than nine years. The U.S. government reported that initial jobless claims rose to 450,000 in the week to Sept. 22, surpassing forecasts for a rise to 429,000. \par \par \uc2  In a separate report, new orders for costly manufactured goods fell for a third straight month, indicating that the economy had been weakening even before the deadly attacks on the United States. Durable goods orders fell 0.3 in August. \par \par \uc2  The data heightened speculation that U.S. rates would be cut again - after eight rate cuts so far this year - amid persistent signs of economic weakness. ECB council members also left the door open for further easing. \par \par \uc2  Two-year note yields fell to new historic lows and five-year note yields hit new 38-year lows. Investors have flocked to government bonds in recent days as a safe haven from an uncertain economic and political future. \par \par \uc2  Two-year notes rose 6/32 at 99-30/32, yielding 2.78 percent. Five-year notes rose 4/32 to 103-22/32, yielding 3.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Ten-year notes were up 20/32 at 103-16/32, yielding 4.56 percent, and 30-year bonds were up 26/32 at 99 to yield 5.44 percent. \par \par \uc2  "When you see the stock market declining and the bond market rising, it's almost always symptomatic of worries about the economy intensifying," First Albany's Johnson said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SOFTENS YEN \par \par \uc2  The dollar surged above 120 yen - about 3.5 percent above seven-month lows around 115.75 yen hit last week - after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with the help of the New York Federal Reserve to stem the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was more than 1.50 percent above its previous U.S. close, trading at 119.65 yen. The euro also rallied against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing above the 110-yen level in early New York trading for the first time since Aug. 23 and then slipping back to trade at 109.83 yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan repeatedly stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday to weaken the yen in an effort to help Japanese exporters and keep deflationary pressure from deepening Japan's economic woes. \par \par \uc2  "They are making their intentions clear, they don't want a rapid appreciation of the yen," said David Mozina, director of global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income research at Bank of America in New York. "They're being quite brash about it." \par \par \uc2  OIL RESUMES REBOUND \par \par \uc2  Oil shares offered the U.S. stock market some support, with crude oil prices heading higher following gains in prices overnight. NYMEX November crude oil futures settled 36 cents higher at $22.74 a barrel. \par \par \uc2  U.S. oil heavyweight Exxon Mobil rose $2.41 to $38.42, bolstering the Dow index, and the American Stock Exchange's oil index jumped 4.48 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9s0040h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
