\par\par \b \uc2  Japan MOF Mutoh:Sudden Yen Rise Would Hurt Econ Recovery \b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 10:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The top bureaucrat at Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday a sudden rise in the value of the yen would hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery and indicated recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is aimed at limiting such effects. \par \par \uc2  "A sudden rise in the value of the yen would have a bad effect on the economy's recovery," said Administrative Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Mutoh at a regular press conference. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday to buy dollars for yen on the MOF's behalf and whether the move - at a time when the market was calm - was inconsistent with the MOF's policy of acting in the event of large market swings, Mutoh said the timing of such moves should be viewed over a longer time span than one day. \par \par \uc2  "It depends on how you view the market and over what time span. Of course there will be daily moves, but there may be a view that the yen is strengthening suddenly over a certain period of time," Mutoh said. \par \par \uc2  "If this is the case, it will have a negative impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic recovery." \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the dollar when it was at around Y117.80 Thursday, successfully lifting the U.S. unit to Y118.60 in a matter of minutes. \par \par \uc2  Persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a yen rise has lifted the dollar from around Y116.00 in recent days. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he meant the government was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market so that a strong yen would not hamper Japan's economic recovery, and not necessarily to maintain market stability, Mutoh said "I don't mean to say differently." \par \par \uc2  "But this doesn't mean that the importance of market stability is inconsistent with the fact that a sudden rise in the yen hurts Japan's economic recovery," Mutoh said. \par \par \uc2  Mutoh added he did not believe that Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's statement in a speech earlier Thursday that he will act "boldly and flexibly" on structural reform meant the premier would yield on his target of capping annual bond issues at Y30 trillion when compiling an extra budget. \par \par \uc2  "Reading that speech objectively, I don't think you can directly link that part to the extra budget," Mutoh said. \par \par \uc2  Koizumi reiterated he intends to stick to his goal of limiting bond issues in a speech opening a special session of Parliament. \par \par \uc2  But he didn't rule out increased spending, saying only "We can't easily resort to increasing bond issuance." He also reiterated he will respond "boldly and flexibly to changing economic conditions" - a remark interpreted by Japanese media and many in the market as a hint of more spending. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Audrey McAvoy, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par audrey.mcavoy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010927dx9r000hk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
