\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa Tells Megawati In Touch With O'Neill \b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 07:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told visiting Indonesian President Megawati Sukarnoputri Tokyo remains in close contact with Washington on economic stability following the U.S. terror attacks. \par \par \uc2  A Japanese finance ministry official, briefing reporters on a meeting between Shiokawa and Megawati held earlier Thursday, quoted Shiokawa as saying that he remains in close touch with U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa made the comments after Megawati informed the Japanese finance minister of her recent trip to the U.S. and her meeting with top officials, including O'Neill, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Megawati also expressed concern over how the Sep. 11 terrorist attacks would affect Japan-U.S. relations, and in turn, Japan's relations with Indonesia, the official said. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa explained that Japan stands ready to support the U.S. in the aftermath of the attacks, according to the official. \par \par \uc2  He added that Shiokawa told Megawati of the Group of Seven nations' telephone conference held earlier this week to discuss economic and financial market stability. In the phone conference, Shiokawa expressed Tokyo's commitment to maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate stability. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, both in Tokyo and in overseas markets through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in order to support the dollar. On Thursday, the MOF and the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, buying dollars for yen and lifting the greenback to a high of Y118.63. \par \par \uc2  In Thursday's 30-minute meeting, Shiokawa also told Megawati that Japan's fiscal situation remains severe and that it will be forced to reconsider its stance on overseas assistance, the official said. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese government has already decided to slash the official development assistance budget by 10% in next fiscal year's budget. \par \par \uc2  On Tokyo's close ties with Indonesia, Japan will aim to provide as much assistance to the country as possible under its fiscal restrictions, both through ODA and credit, the official quoted Shiokawa as saying. \par \par \uc2  Megawati told Shiokawa that her Cabinet is committed to resolving long-standing issues in need of attention, especially economic issues, the Japanese official said. \par \par \uc2  The two top economic priorities will be to address investment in the country and Indonesia's debts, Megawati was quoted as saying. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par -0- 27/09/01 05-30G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010927dx9r000a1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
