\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japan, with help of ECB and NY Fed, sends yen reeling. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 23:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The yen fell to a two-week low against the dollar and a one-month trough versus the euro on Thursday, thanks to Japan's steely determination to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the sixth time in 10 days. \par \par \uc2  The sixth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Sept. 17 was even more notable in that Japanese officials said they enlisted the aid of the New York Federal Reserve during U.S. trading, as well as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) during London trading hours. \par \par \uc2  The New York Fed declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  Japan's monetary authorities want a weak yen because it makes the country's exports cheaper abroad. It also represents one of the few policy options left to a government trying to navigate the shoals of a protracted economic slump. \par \par \uc2  "The last thing Japan needs economically is an appreciating yen," said Nic Pifer, portfolio manager at American Express Asset Management in Minneapolis. "When you have a country that runs a current account surplus and it's not getting adequately recycled by private investors, either the yen strengthens as it (has), or the public sector comes in and does the recycling for it." \par \par \uc2  After initially shooting above 120 yen, the dollar bought 119.65 yen at the close of the U.S. trading day, still more than 1.50 percent higher than its previous U.S. close and about 2 yen above the day's lows. The greenback is now 4 units above last week's near seven-month lows. \par \par \uc2  The euro finished the session around 109.80 yen, clambering off its peaks at 110.20 but up more than 1.10 percent on the day and its strongest since August 23. \par \par \uc2  Risk-averse investors seeking safe-haven in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries with large current-account surpluses - i.e. Japan-and investors repatriating assets ahead of a fiscal half-year end on Sept. 30 have been the principal contributors to the yen's recent strengthening. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN UPS THE ANTE TO MARKET \par \par \uc2  Starting in Asian trading, Japanese authorities repeatedly stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the yen in an attempt to prevent deflationary pressure from worsening in the world's second-biggest economy, and to help manufacturers as Japan teeters on the brink of recession. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it feared the yen's recent surge could harm prospects for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  While Japan's increasingly aggressive forays into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have made traders nervous about buying yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said it may be just a matter of time before the dollar starts weakening again. \par \par \uc2  "We remain concerned that capital inflows into the U.S. from Japan will be insufficient to cover the current account imbalance, and that dollar/yen will fall over a three-month horizon," said Robert Sinche, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  WEAK U.S. DATA WEIGHS ON FRAGILE DOLLAR SENTIMENT \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the yen helped the greenback keep the euro pinned near 91.70 cents at the close, down half a percent from its previous U.S. close. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded around 1.6130 Swiss francs, up nearly 0.90 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  However, pervasive unease over the halting U.S. economy prevented the dollar from making significant headway as battered Wall Street shares shrugged off earlier losses. \par \par \uc2  Initial jobless claims surged to 450,000 - the highest level in more than nine years - in the week following the attacks. U.S. durable goods orders were also disappointing, falling 0.3 percent in August. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's a sign of things to come. Nobody's hiring and everybody's laying off," said Joe Francomano, vice president and strategic trader at Erste Bank in New York. "Ultimately, the dollar should weaken over the course of the next quarter." \par \par \uc2  Earlier, the ECB left interest rates steady at its regular monetary policy meeting on Thursday, as had been expected after the ECB's inter-meeting cut, along with a Fed rate reduction, on Sept. 17. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010928dx9r00eow\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
