\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-U.S. stocks slip further, bonds gain. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Elizabeth Lazarowitz \par \uc2 1,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 19:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 27 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks stumbled lower on Thursday and U.S. Treasuries rose for a third straight day as the steady drone of corporate profit warnings hit the high-tech sector and nagging worries about global recession unnerved investors. \par \par \uc2  The threat of a likely U.S. military response to the Sept. 11 attacks in the United States also hung over the U.S. stock market, which trudged steadily downward, failing to match the modest gains eked out in European exchanges. \par \par \uc2  "The evidence is mounting that the world changed on Sept. 11. and the (U.S.) economy went into a recession," said Hugh Johnson, chief investment officer at First Albany Corp. "The message of the markets continues to be that there is no recovery in the economy and profits in sight." \par \par \uc2  The yen slumped to fresh two-week lows against the dollar and a new one-month trough vs. the euro after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to stave off yen strength authorities there fear will damage prospects for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Bonds rose, pushing yields on some shorter-term issues to historic lows, after fresh data pointed to deepening economic troubles and investors bet the U.S. Federal Reserve would respond with further interest rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices marched higher, however, resuming a rebound begun on Wednesday that broke seven straight days of decline amid fears that faltering global economic health would crimp demand for gasoline and other products. \par \par \uc2  TECHNOLOGY LEADS STOCK SELL-OFF \par \par \uc2  High-tech shares lead the U.S. stock market's drop amid a slew of profit warnings from the likes of network equipment maker Sonus Networks Inc., which said late Wednesday it would post a loss in the current quarter as sales fall 25 percent below expectations. \par \par \uc2  Sonus shares fell $4 to $2.40 on Nasdaq. \par \par \uc2  The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index dropped 41.83 points, or 2.86 percent, to 1,422.21, further eroding the rebound eked out earlier this week in the wake of last week's brutal sell-off. In midday trading, the index scraped out a session low at 1,418.15. \par \par \uc2  Blue-chip shares followed suit, sending the Dow Jones industrial average down 64.48 points, or 0.75 percent, at 8,502.91, while the broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index sagged 6.16 points, or 0.61 percent, to 1,000.88. \par \par \uc2  "This whole market is now focused on earnings downgrades, and so far we have seen twice as many as we have seen in the same quarter last year," said Thom Brown of Walnut Asset Management, which oversees $700 million. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets eked out slim gains, bolstered by strength in energy and health care heavyweights, but softness in technology shares held back their advance. Traders largely shrugged of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave interest rates steady, as expected. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 rose 1.3 percent, and the narrower DJ Euro Stoxx 50 rose 1.11 percent. \par \par \uc2  Overnight, Asian stock markets gained ground, as strength in select blue-chip firms bolstered Japanese stocks. Tokyo's key Nikkei 225 average rose 0.57 percent to 9,696.53, while Hong Kong's Hang Seng index rose 2.44 percent to 9,600.79. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC OUTLOOK DARKENS \par \par \uc2  Worries about the economy grew after the number of American signing up for U.S. jobless benefits hit their highest level in more than nine years. The U.S. government reported initial jobless claims rose to 450,000 in the week to Sept. 22, surpassing forecasts for a rise to 429,000. \par \par \uc2  In a separate report, new orders for costly manufactured goods fell for a third straight month, indicating the economy was weakening even before the attacks on the United States. Durable goods orders fell 0.3 in August. \par \par \uc2  The data heightened speculation that U.S. rates would be cut again - after eight rate cuts so far this year - amid persistent signs of economic weakness, while ECB council members also left the door open for further easing to counter global recession. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury prices surged again on Thursday as traders bet on further Fed easing, pushing two-year note yields to new historic lows and five-year note yields to new 38-year lows. Investors have flocked to government bonds in recent days as a safe haven from an uncertain economic and political future. \par \par \uc2  Two-year notes were up 8/32 at 99-31/32, yielding 2.76 percent. Five-year notes rose 7/32 to 103-25/32, yielding 3.73 percent. \par \par \uc2  Ten-year notes were up 16/32 at 103-15/32, yielding 4.56 percent, and 30-year bonds were up 28/32 at 98-27/32 to yield 5.45 percent. \par \par \uc2  "When you see the stock market declining and the bond market rising it's almost always symptomatic of worries about the economy intensifying," First Albany's Johnson said. \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SOFTENS YEN \par \par \uc2  Within an hour after senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi confirmed the latest round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar had extended its gains above 120 yen JPY=), about 3.5 percent above seven-month lows around 115.75 yen hit last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 119.70 yen. The euro also rallied against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing above the 110 yen level in early New York trading for the first time since Aug. 23 and then slipping back to trade at 109.95 yen. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan repeatedly stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday to weaken the yen in an effort to help Japanese exporters and keep deflationary pressure from deepening Japan's economic woes. \par \par \uc2  "They are making their intentions clear, they don't want a rapid appreciation of the yen," said David Mozina, director of global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income research at Bank of America in New York. "They're being quite brash about it." \par \par \uc2  OIL RESUMES REBOUND \par \par \uc2  Oil shares offered the U.S. stock market some support, with crude oil prices heading higher after gains in prices overnight. NYMEX November crude oil futures were up 37 cents at $22.75. \par \par \uc2  U.S. oil heavyweight Exxon Mobil rose $1.07 to $37.08, and the American Stock Exchange's oil index rose 1.64 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00xi3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
