\par\par \b \uc2  CHRONOLOGY - History of  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 16:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Following is a chronology of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by central banks from major industrial nations on the dollar, the yen, the German mark and the euro. \par \par \uc2  Sept 17, 19, 21, 24, 26 and 27, 2001 - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars, worrying about an export-crippling rise in the value of the yen and following attacks on U.S. cities on Sept 11. Sept. 24 and 26/27 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not limited to the dollar but includes buying euros for yen, with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) and euro zone central banks operating on behalf of BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Sept. 27 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  says the New York Federal Reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf for the first time in the September round of yen selling. \par \par \uc2  Nov 10, 2000 - ECB in conjunction with national euro zone central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  Nov 6, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros. \par \par \uc2  Nov 3, 2000 - ECB and other euro zone national central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least twice to buy euros, following the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five percent recovery from record lows set the previous week around $0.8225. \par \par \uc2  Sept 22, 2000 - Central banks in Europe, Japan and the United States, acting together for first time since 1995, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive euro higher after \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits all-time low below 85 cents two days earlier, a loss of nearly 30 percent of its value since its January 1999 launch. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is first by ECB since its birth. \par \par \uc2  January 1999 to April 2000 - Japan concerned too-strong yen, trading around 108 to the dollar in January, 1999, will choke off fragile economic recovery and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to tame its strength. Bank of Japan (BOJ) sells yen at least 18 times in this time period, including once via the Federal Reserve and once via the ECB. Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , yen continues to strengthen against dollar, reaching 102 by April 2000. \par \par \uc2  April-June 1998 - With yen weakening, BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As yen crumples below 144 to the dollar, U.S. authorities on June 17, 1998 join the BOJ, spending $833 million buying yen. \par \par \uc2  April 1994 - August 1995 - Dollar sinks to record lows against the German mark, reaching 1.41 in July 1995, and it hits post-World-War Two lows against the yen below 83 to the dollar in April 1995. From April 1994, United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly, often in concert with Japanese and individual European central banks, to prop up U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The last coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with European banks in this period takes place on August 15, 1995. \par \par \uc2  April-August 1993 - U.S. buys dollars and sells yen. \par \par \uc2  1991-1992 - U.S. and European central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly as U.S. economy tumbles into recession during Gulf War, which weakens the dollar. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on both sides of market, spending more than $2.5 billion buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and selling $750 million. \par \par \uc2  1988-1990 - Dollar trends higher. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  after Group of Seven statements on importance of maintaining exchange rate stability. \par \par \uc2  February 1987 - Louvre Accord. Weak dollar, growing U.S. trade deficit and prospect of weakening U.S. economy raises concerns in Europe and Japan about further dollar weakening. G5 plus Italy meet at the Louvre in Paris and issue statement agreeing to "foster stability of exchange rates around current levels." United States, frequently in coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  a number of times to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  September 1985 - Plaza Accord. Group of Five - U.S., Germany, Japan, Britain, France - meet at Plaza Hotel in New York to discuss concerns about very strong dollar and U.S. worries about declining competitiveness. Following weeks see substantial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales of dollars for other G5 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S. and other monetary authorities. \par \par \uc2  1980-1981 - U.S. authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tame strengthened dollar. \par \par \uc2  1978-1979 - Dollar comes under heavy pressure amid high oil prices, high U.S. inflation and deteriorating balance of payments. In November 1978 a major new dollar support program is introduced to raise a large war chest of up to $30 billion. U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  forcefully and often in the market with other central banks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00qvc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*