\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen keeps low profile after BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Natsuko Waki \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The yen held more than one percent lower against the dollar and the euro on Thursday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third time this week to halt export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed the Bank of Japan bought dollars for yen in Tokyo and European trading, pushing the yen to two-week lows against the greenback and a one-month trough against the euro. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it and euro zone central banks bought euros for yen on Japan's behalf. \par \par \uc2  The euro was a third of a percent down on the buoyant dollar, showing little reaction after the ECB left interest rates unchanged as expected. However many believe its long-term prospects are positive. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is trying to get the best dollar/yen level ahead of book closing on Friday. It seems 120 yen might be a comfortable level for both the U.S. and Japan," Halifax treasury economist Nick Mirtchev said. \par \par \uc2  "The euro should be supported in the long term, the eurozone economy is looking less bad compared to Japan and the outlook for the United States is not good after the attacks." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose nearly 1.5 percent against the yen to hit two-week highs around 119.38, off three percent from a seven-month low set last week. \par \par \uc2  The euro bounded to a one-month high against the yen of 109.89 from the New York close around 108.62. \par \par \uc2  The euro edged down against the dollar to $0.9194 from the New York close around $0.9230. \par \par \uc2  "What the BOJ has been doing for this week is to try to let the market know they are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they have been quite successful," Henry Wilkes, head of European \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  "Some market feeling is that the BOJ may not be finished until the dollar/yen goes up above 120. So the market is definitely nervous." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CAUTION \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is battling to restrain the yen to prevent deflationary pressure from worsening and to help domestic manufacturers at a time when the economy risks tipping into a recession. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say a weaker yen could be a boost to exports. It is also one of the few viable options for action by the Japanese authorities, given their limited scope in terms of fiscal and monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it feared the yen's surge could harm prospects for economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ must have bought a few billion (dollars). In the short term the risk is on the upside, but we don't know how the dollar/yen would go after book closing on Friday," a London-based Japanese bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the BOJ's efforts, general sentiment on the dollar remained frail with the Dow Jones industrial average falling 1.1 percent on Wednesday as warnings about corporate earnings snapped a two-day relief rally. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday U.S. economic growth was skidding lower before the September 11 attacks and now it was impossible to predict what would happen. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa did admit yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was having limited impact. "The top side (in dollar/yen) is heavy and it hasn't broken through it. On the other hand, it is not going down either," Shiokawa told a government panel. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said repatriation flows by Japanese investors ahead of half-year book closing in September have almost dried up, which has removed one factor to drive the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  ECB RATE DECISION \par \par \uc2  The ECB left its benchmark minimum bid rate unchanged at 3.75 percent, in line with market expectations, following a 50-basis point cut in reaction to the U.S. attacks on September 11. \par \par \uc2  "This is not surprising as they cut rates last week. We still think there is 50 basis points worth of rate cuts on the agenda by the end of the year," said Tom Vosa, market economist at National Australia Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Other economists also expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates again later this year as signs of gloom in the euro zone economy become more evident. \par \par \uc2  They also said the deteriorating economic outlook and weakening inflationary pressures will take centre stage in ECB thinking in coming weeks, pushing still buoyant money supply even further towards the wings. \par \par \uc2  Eurozone M3 money supply growth, which the ECB sees as a key barometer of future inflation, accelerated faster than expected in August to some two points above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 4.5 percent reference value. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00ojt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
