\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says it again  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to weaken yen. \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 17:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the sixth time in the last two weeks on Thursday to try to weaken the yen in an attempt to limit the damage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength was causing its exporters. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said both the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the New York Federal Reserve had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought euros for yen in Europe for the third time this week, the ministry said. \par \par \uc2  The yen, which had been strengthening recently as investors repatriate assets before the fiscal half-year end and on growing risk aversion, fell to a two-week low against the dollar and a one-month low against the euro after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action. \par \par \uc2  At 1443 GMT, the dollar was trading around 119.80 yen , around 2 yen higher than Wednesday's New York close and 4 yen above the seven-month low of 115.76 reached on September 20. The euro was trading around 110.20 yen , more than one percent above the New York close. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said after the first two bouts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia that the action was not just directed at pushing up the dollar against the yen. \par \par \uc2  "It's not about pushing it up, it's about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability." \par \par \uc2  FUNDAMENTALS NOT REFLECTED \par \par \uc2  Earlier, senior MOF official Zembei Mizoguchi said the yen's rise did not reflect economic fundamentals and warned that Japan would continue to take appropriate action as needed. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it feared the yen's surge could harm prospects for the country's economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  He denied this conflicted with Shiokawa's assertion that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was directed at maintaining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. \par \par \uc2  "Preventing a negative impact on the economy and achieving curency stability don't conflict," he said, adding that officials considered recent moves to be too rapid. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the latest day's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggested \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was doing much more than just drawing a line in the sand, preventing the dollar falling below 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  They pointed to minutes of a meeting of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi top economic panel held last Friday in which Shiokawa complained that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until then had limited impact. \par \par \uc2  "We have already done it four times." \par \par \uc2  "The top side (in dollar/yen) is heavy and it hasn't broken through it. On the other hand, it's not going down either." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00mj9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
