\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Asian assets mixed, wary on U.S. retaliation risk. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Sabyasachi Mitra \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 12:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      HONG KONG, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Asian assets were mixed on \par \par \uc2  Thursday with major regional stock markets eking out gains as \par \par \uc2  investors stayed cautious on uncertainty about retaliation for \par \par \uc2  the suicide strikes in the United States this month. \par \par \uc2      The dollar powered to a two-week high against the yen after \par \par \uc2  the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday for a fifth day in \par \par \uc2  two weeks and enlisted the help of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2      Tokyo share trade, dulled by half-yearly bookclosings, saw \par \par \uc2  the benchmark Nikkei 225 edge up 0.57 percent while the \par \par \uc2  capital-weighted TOPIX index tacked on 0.55 percent. \par \par \uc2      "Trade was directionless pre-bookclosing, though, and \par \par \uc2  concerns about U.S. retaliation and its economy kept buying in \par \par \uc2  check," said Masatoshi Sato, manager at the equity division of \par \par \uc2  Mizuho Investors Securities. \par \par \uc2      Position squaring to cut risk ahead of a long weekend in \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong was the preoccupation of investors there, with the \par \par \uc2  Hang Seng Index gaining 2.4 percent from a late surge in \par \par \uc2  property stocks, while 2.6 percent was added to Singapore's \par \par \uc2  Straits Times Index. \par \par \uc2      TELECOM, TECH DRAGS KOREA, TAIWAN \par \par \uc2      But shares were weaker in Taiwan, and South Korean shares \par \par \uc2  were hit by heavy losses on telecom equities which balanced \par \par \uc2  gains in other sectors. \par \par \uc2      Seoul closed a shade lower as telecom shares extended their \par \par \uc2  losses on profit-taking after recent gains. \par \par \uc2      SK Telecom, the nation's top mobile carrier, ended down \par \par \uc2  3.24 percent on foreign selling while state-run Korea Telecom \par \par \uc2  shed 2.98 percent. \par \par \uc2      The benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) \par \par \uc2  finished down 0.10 percent. \par \par \uc2      "The market is not likely to fall further, as blue chips \par \par \uc2  would soon attract bargain hunting," said Kang In-ho, fund \par \par \uc2  manager at Cheil Investment Trust Management which oversees 1.3 \par \par \uc2  trillion won ($1 billion). \par \par \uc2  A decline Wednesday in the Nasdaq and the slashing of \par \par \uc2  earning forecasts for some key technology shares by Goldman \par \par \uc2  Sachs dragged down Taiwan shares. \par \par \uc2  The key index fell more than two percent before buying by \par \par \uc2  state-linked funds pared losses. The benchmark TAIEX ended down \par \par \uc2  1.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  POWERS DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were slightly weaker after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to soften the yen. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment continued to be cautious in the relatively \par \par \uc2  illiquid regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as the world awaits \par \par \uc2  America's next steps to avenge this month's attacks on New \par \par \uc2  York's twin towers and the Pentagon, which left more than 6,000 \par \par \uc2  people missing or dead. \par \par \uc2  Gloom over the region's economic prospects was underscored \par \par \uc2  after the International Monetary Fund said most of Asia would \par \par \uc2  be hit hard by the global slowdown and the potential fallout of \par \par \uc2  the September 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  Activity could perk up next week, especially with a \par \par \uc2  possible rate cut by the U.S. Federal Reserve following its \par \par \uc2  Federal Open Market Committee meeting on October 2. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE BONDS IN FOCUS \par \par \uc2  Singapore bonds were in focus after United Overseas Bank \par \par \uc2  issued $1.27 billion worth of subordinated debt in a private \par \par \uc2  placement with a unit of U.S. investment bank JPMorgan. \par \par \uc2  The deal, announced late on Wednesday, sparked some selling \par \par \uc2  of Singapore banks' subordinated debt in New York trade, but \par \par \uc2  the pressure receded in the Asian session on Thursday, traders \par \par \uc2  said. \par \par \uc2      Bonds issued by DBS Bank, the largest dollar denominated \par \par \uc2  sub-debt issuer in the region, were in focus. \par \par \uc2      Spreads on DBS '11s stabilised at around 228 basis points \par \par \uc2  over U.S. Treasuries after moving out by some eight basis \par \par \uc2  points (bps) to 227 bps in New York trade. \par \par \uc2      Subordinated bonds of OCBC due in 2011 were quoted at 270 \par \par \uc2  bps over Treasuries, against 265 bps previously. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. $0901 GMT \par \par \uc2  Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     118.63       117.30         -1.12\par  Sing dlr      1.7612       1.7693         +0.46\par  Taiwan dlr     34.52        34.57         +0.15\par  Korean won   1304.25      1302.00         -0.17\par  Baht           44.35        44.39         +0.09\par  Peso           51.34        51.30         -0.08\par  Rupiah       9655.00      9620.00         -0.36\par  Ringgit       3.7995       3.7994         -0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f2