\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  fails to calm  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  investor nerves. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 858 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BOJ) relentless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar and restrain the yen has so far failed to restore Japanese investors' confidence in foreign assets. \par \par \uc2  The twin spectres of war and recession have predictably led to fears that the dollar, and by extension U.S. assets, will need more than just a helping hand from the BOJ to stay afloat in the coming months. \par \par \uc2  "There are many uncertainties. No one can be sure what is going to happen next. At a time like this, we'd better play safe," said a portfolio manager at a major Japanese life insurance firm. \par \par \uc2  But rather than hitting the panic button, Japanese investors are adopting a cautious approach, mindful that a military crisis has not always been bad news for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign has been a mixed success so far, stopping the yen from rising above 115 per dollar, but failing to push the dollar up to pre-attack levels. \par \par \uc2  As Japan looks set enter its fourth recession in a decade in the second half of this year, Tokyo is anxious to avoid a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would pile on the pain for exporters. \par \par \uc2  Market players estimate the BOJ has sold around two trillion yen ($16.97 billion) during the past two weeks to buy dollars and, to a lesser extent, euros. \par \par \uc2  But Japanese institutional investors are worried that sentiment for the dollar is unlikely to recover any time soon as the United States girds for a "war on terrorism" that even President George W. Bush has admitted could take years to finish. \par \par \uc2  GULF WAR RERUN? \par \par \uc2  "Military spending may grow by more than expected. Another terror attack might happen," said the portfolio manager, explaining why he was remaining wary of dollar assets. \par \par \uc2  Some investors, however, think the dollar's fall could be temporary, drawing lessons from their experience during the Gulf War a decade ago. For a few months after Iraq invaded Kuwait in August 1990, the dollar fell from around 150 yen to 124 yen. \par \par \uc2  But it rebounded a few months before Washington and its allies began bombing Baghdad in January 1991, on the hope that overwhelming military strength would enable the United States to win the war quickly and at a relatively cheap cost. \par \par \uc2  Others are not so sure that this parallel can be drawn with the brewing crisis in Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  In contrast to the relatively straightforward objectives in 1990, the United States now faces hidden enemies in a harsh mountainous country that two superpowers, Great Britain in the 19th century and Russia in the 20th century, failed to tame. \par \par \uc2  And while the military outlook remains uncertain, most investors are sure the outlook for the U.S. economy will also be poor, a major factor weighing on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Weak U.S. economic fundamentals deteriorated further after the terror attacks on the U.S., and the dollar will be vulnerable for the time being," said Yasuhiro Miyata, investment planning manager at Dai-Ichi Mutual Life Insurance Co. \par \par \uc2  U.S. consumer confidence fell sharply in September, suffering its largest one-month drop since October 1990 - during the Gulf crisis - according to a survey that captured part of the economic shock from the attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  "Only consumption has been supporting the U.S. economy but now consumer confidence is fading. So we are unlikely to see signs of a U.S. recovery perhaps until next April," said Miyata of Dai-Ichi Life. \par \par \uc2  "Until then, it's difficult to buy dollars," he added. \par \par \uc2  Despite this, fears of a huge repatriation of Japanese funds ahead of end-September bookclosings might be overblown. \par \par \uc2  Although the U.S. stock market has tumbled alarmingly since the attacks, the Treasury market seems to have retained some of its status as a safe haven. Treasury prices soared for a second session on Wednesday on hopes for more rate cuts, with short-term notes continuing to attract particularly strong demand. \par \par \uc2  PAST \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\uc2  SUCCESSFUL \par \par \uc2  And while many in the market are sceptical that the BOJ can reverse the dollar's weak trend, a few investors are allowing themselves some bullishness, arguing that BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past few years have been fairly successful. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think market players expect to win a battle against \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Michihisa Tanimoto, deputy general manager of the securities investment department at Sumitomo Life Insurance Co. \par \par \uc2  Because the BOJ is guiding short-term rates near zero, the cost of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - in which the Japanese government borrows yen to buy dollars - is almost nothing, and Japan's coffers are almost infinitely deep, Tanimoto said. \par \par \uc2  "With interest rates at zero, Japan can in effect print as much yen as possible and sell it for dollars." \par \par \uc2  Furthermore, investors selling the dollar for yen receive no interest while they lose an opportunity to earn interest on dollars, he added. \par \par \uc2  "I think the dollar at 120 to 130 yen is a comfortable range for both Japanese and U.S. authorities, and the dollar will return to that range eventually," Tanimoto said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00had\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
