\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar powers higher as BOJ leans on yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 10:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The dollar powered to its highest level in nearly two weeks against the yen on Thursday, pumped up by the third bout of Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  The greenback gained a third of a percent against the euro ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's regular interest rate announcement later on Thursday and clawed back more than half a percent against the Swiss franc as the market took profits on long positions in the safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , determinedly bought dollars for yen in thin lunchtime trade in Tokyo, with continuous follow-up action that triggered buying above 118.00 yen and pushed the dollar to a two-week high of 118.70. \par \par \uc2  "Now the dollar looks in far better shape having got above 118.50 yen and it's the first really encouraging sign," said Tony Norfield, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ABN Amro. \par \par \uc2  "But the reason why you would be a little cautious saying the dollar's turned is that the source of demand is Japanese officials rather than the free market," he added. \par \par \uc2  Going forward, the market would continue to be wary of BOJ buying, especially as Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa warned Japan would continue to take action "if needed" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Japan is battling to restrain the yen in order to prevent deflationary pressure from worsening and to help domestic manufacturers. \par \par \uc2  However the yen has been supported by repatriation of foreign funds ahead of this month's half-year end book closing. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading near the day's highs around 118.75 yen by 0832 GMT, almost a percent higher on its New York close and about 2.5 percent above a seven-month low set last week. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the dollar was trading at $0.9210, a third of a percent stronger on the day, while against the Swiss franc it was trading at 1.6072 francs, more than 2.5 percent above last week's 20-month trough. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to a one-week high around 109.43 yen and was up more than a quarter percent up on the Swiss franc at 1.4801 francs. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CAUTION \par \par \uc2  Tokyo dealers said the dollar had been well-supported since early Asian trade, with many dealers placing bids around 117.50 yen on expectations that the BOJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after it came in to stem the yen's strength via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, acting through the ECB for the second time this week. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the BOJ stepped in to buy dollars from around 117.40 yen surprised the market, so many dealers had prepared for more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  today," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the BOJ's effort, general sentiment on the dollar remained frail with the Dow Jones industrial average falling 1.1 percent on Wednesday as warnings about corporate earnings snapped a two-day relief rally. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday that U.S. economic growth was skidding lower before the September 11 attacks and now it was impossible to predict what would happen. \par \par \uc2  Under these circumstances, the U.S. Federal Reserve was expected to cut its Federal funds rate by 50 basis points at next week's policy-setting meeting. \par \par \uc2  Many cash-rich Japanese institutional investors were trying to make decisions on what they should do with their money in the second half of the fiscal year, with some looking for an opportunity to buy U.S. dollar-denominated bonds, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said investors were showing interest in buying U.S. bonds of maturities of between two and five years but looking for more clues on the U.S. economic outlook and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  A bond trader at a Japanese bank said investors were also looking at U.S. corporate bonds with credit ratings above A grade with spreads more than 50 basis points above Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  ECB RATE DECISION \par \par \uc2  The ECB holds its regular monetary policy meeting on Thursday with no change in interest rates expected after its intra-meeting cut alongside the U.S. Federal Reserve on September 17. \par \par \uc2  The ECB lowered its benchmark minimum bid rate by 50 basis points to 3.75 percent in reaction to the devastating attacks on the United States on September 11. \par \par \uc2  However many economists polled by Reuters do expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates again later this year with signs of gloom in the euro zone economy coming from French business confidence, which slumped more than expected in September, and September Dutch consumer confidence, which was hard hit in the aftershock of the U.S. attacks. \par \par \uc2  The ECB rate decision is due at 1145 GMT with no news conference scheduled. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, U.S. data due include weekly jobless claims and August durable goods orders at 1230 GMT. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00goa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
