\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares edge higher despite uncertainty. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 949 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 09:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 27 (Reuters) - Major Asian share markets chalked up moderate gains on Thursday but darkening economic clouds and uncertainty over the course of Washington's war against terrorism put a firm lid on the advance. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was in the limelight, climbing sharply against the yen after another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to head off a rise in the yen that would intensify deflationary pressure in Japan and further hurt its ailing exporters. \par \par \uc2  "Uncertainty is the market's only tone," said Hans Kunnen, head of investment markets research at Colonial First State Fund Managers in Sydney, which oversees some US$20 billion in assets. \par \par \uc2  Still, as was the case the day before, Asian equities held up quite well considering that the Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped 1.07 percent to 8,567.39 on Wednesday and the Nasdaq composite fell 2.50 percent to 1,464.04. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's key Nikkei 225 average closed 0.57 percent higher at 9,696.53, helped by selected blue-chip issues. \par \par \uc2  "There's uncertainty in the market and investors are looking to large-cap shares for relative safety," said Masatoshi Sato, manager at the equity division of Mizuho Investors Securities. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng index rose 1.03 percent to 9,468.68 in mid-afternoon and Singapore's Straits Times index added 1.37 percent to 1,295.20. \par \par \uc2  Jayesh Shroff, fund manager at BOB Asset Management Company in Bombay, where shares were up 0.41 percent, said sentiment was marginally better as traders were cautiously hopeful that U.S. reprisals for the deadly attacks on New York and Washington might not escalate into a full-scale war. \par \par \uc2  "Confidence is returning to the market as the recent U.S. statements indicate that there may not be a full-blown war," he said. \par \par \uc2  But analysts across the region said the markets remained fragile because of mounting evidence of a global recession and the likelihood of a big hit to corporate earnings. \par \par \uc2  "There will be technical rebounds along the way, but the overall trend is still downwards at this point because of the global uncertainties arising from the attacks," said a dealer at a local brokerage in Kuala Lumpur, where shares were up 0.85 percent. \par \par \uc2  GRIM ECONOMIC NEWS \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the parade of grim economic news showed no sign of ending. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said the pulse of the global economy, already weak before the shock of the September 11 attacks, had slowed further, while a U.S. poll by Roper ASW said consumer confidence slipped to recessionary levels in September. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, brokerage analysts cut their earnings expectations for computer giant International Business Machines and chip maker Intel Corp. \par \par \uc2  One ray of hope for the global economy was a decision by OPEC producers to rule out bigger oil output curbs that might have risked deepening the world's economic downturn by raising the cost of energy supplies. \par \par \uc2  At a short closed-door meeting in Vienna ministers agreed that, with demand ebbing fast as the world economy weakens, they had no option but to keep output for 10 member countries steady at 23.2 million barrels a day, according to delegates. \par \par \uc2  After a brief spike following the attacks on the United States, U.S. crude slumped nearly 27 percent to two-year lows before finding a floor earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  "OPEC has done the only thing possible. And I applaud them for not doing anything rash," said Bob Finch, head of trading at Vitol in London. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark U.S. November crude futures rose 50 cents to $22.88 a barrel in late New York electronic trading after chalking up 57 cents during open-outcry trading earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN SELLS THE YEN \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, dealers said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) appeared to be in the market continuously buying dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a two-week high of 118.70 yen, up a full yen from New York's close on Wednesday, after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  caught dealers off guard by stepping in to buy the dollar during thin lunchtime trade. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to push the dollar decisively higher," said Takashi Toyahara, manager of the fixed-income section at Nomura Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi told parliament on Thursday that Japan owed it to the world to revive its economy after a decade of stagnation, an already thankless task that economists said would become even tougher if the yen were to surge. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the BOJ could struggle to keep the brakes on the yen. "There will be limits to a dollar rise... The likely recession in the U.S. economy and the impact on U.S. stocks aren't fully reflected in the market yet," Masayuki Yamamoto, research analyst at Bank of America, suggested. \par \par \uc2  Gold and the Swiss franc were losing some attraction, falling modestly in Asian dealings having gained persistently since the air attacks on the United States because they are seen as a safe haven for investment in times of crisis. \par \par \uc2  Gold slipped to around US$291.65/2.25 an ounce after ending New York trading around $293.40. \par \par \uc2  The dollar advanced sharply to 1.6063/69 francs from 1.5990 in late offshore trading on Wednesday and was more than three centimes better than Friday's 20-month lows. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has cut interest rates by a full percentage point in September to head off a surge of safe-haven flows into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00da3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
