\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan currency intervention \highlight\uc2  turns textbook on head. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 855 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - A burst of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days has pumped two trillion yen ($17 billion) or more into money markets here, prompting hopes in some quarters that it will help boost the stagnant economy. \par \par \uc2  But a majority of economists and traders say this theory, while fine in textbooks, is bunk in a country where interest rates and economic confidence are already near zero. \par \par \uc2  Traders estimate the Bank of Japan has spent two trillion yen or more buying dollars for yen in several rounds of unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last week. The main aim has been to prevent an export-threatening rise in the yen as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reels from the shocking attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  Judging by the amount that was left in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account deposits, traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unsterilised - in other words, the funds used to buy dollars were left in the market rather than being absorbed by the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  This has given the Bank some extra breathing space as it fends off criticism from politicians and some economists that it is still not doing enough to prevent the economy sliding into a fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  In an annual report published in June, the Bank for International Settlements (BIS) joined the chorus calling for the BOJ to unsterilise its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , urging the bank to take more risk to stimulate the economy. \par \par \uc2  RED HERRING \par \par \uc2  But how much impact is it really having? \par \par \uc2  For one thing, the amount of liquidity generated by unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just a drop in the ocean of funds it is already supplying under its quantitative easing policy. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ currently leaves about 50 trillion yen worth of credit in the banking system each day through money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as purchases of treasury and commercial bills. \par \par \uc2  That compares with dollar-buying of between $2 billion and $10 billion when it has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days. \par \par \uc2  "The amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very limited in size compared with the mega-liquidity the BOJ provides through other channels," said Shin Nagai, treasurer at ABN AMRO Bank N.V. \par \par \uc2  What's more, economists say most of those funds are sitting idle because firms are too busy struggling with debt and anaemic demand to think about taking out new loans. \par \par \uc2  "The rise in the monetary base has little economic significance in this abnormal environment where companies' demand for funds is sluggish and banks are not making new loans," said Yasushi Okada, Chief Economist at Credit Suisse First Boston Securities (Japan) Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "The increased monetary base only adds to the piles of unused, idle money that banks hold at the BOJ." \par \par \uc2  BOJ data shows that funds deposited with it by commercial banks beyond their reserve requirement stood at 6.0 trillion yen on Wednesday, up from around 1.8 trillion yen a month ago. \par \par \uc2  The extra amount in these non-interest-bearing deposits represents surplus funds for the banking sector, which under normal economic growth conditions would be used as additional reserves against which banks extend extra loans. \par \par \uc2  However, Japanese bank lending marked a 44th straight month of year-on-year decline in August, suggesting that the extra money has indeed been laying idle. \par \par \uc2  ROCK-BOTTOM RATES \par \par \uc2  In theory, unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also help create a surplus of funds in the banking system and thereby increase downward pressure on interest rates. But with Japan's overnight rate already at 0.002 percent, there is precious little room for further rate falls. \par \par \uc2  "I see little point in calling for unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Nagai. "In \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  there is absolutely no scope for a further rate decline." \par \par \uc2  To be effective, unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to be conducted in a more normal economy where short-term interest rates were around 3-5 percent, he said. \par \par \uc2  Only last week, the BOJ loosened its monetary policy further, pledging to pump more than two trillion yen into the market every day and cutting the official discount rate to a record low 0.10 percent from 0.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  UNSTABLE POLICY TOOL \par \par \uc2  Aside from its doubtful impact on the economy, traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a far from stable policy tool as it can disrupt markets in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Massive fund injections by the Federal Reserve drove down short-term dollar interest rates to near zero for a while after the terror attacks. \par \par \uc2  But after the BOJ stepped into the market, overnight dollar LIBOR (the London Interbank Offered Rate) shot up to near four percent on Monday from 1.9 percent in the middle of last week. \par \par \uc2  It was at 3.25 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "This is tantamount to soaking up dollar funds given by the Fed, albeit temporarily and partially," said Okada of CSFB. \par \par \uc2  Others say the steep rise in dollar LIBOR was caused by the Fed itself as it gradually returns liquidity to normal. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, traders say the BOJ's large-lot dollar buying from the market pushes up volatility in the dollar money market particularly with trade so thin after the attacks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00cch\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
