\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar muscles up on continuing BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 869 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The dollar powered up decisively above 118 yen in Tokyo on Thursday after the Bank of Japan sold the yen in thin lunchtime trade and triggered heavy stop-loss buying in the greenback, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be buying dollars continuously during the afternoon to push home its advantage. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spiked up to a two-week high of 118.70 yen as of 0600 GMT, swallowing up major offers lined up between 118.00 and 118.30 yen, traders said. In late U.S. trade on Wednesday it was at 117.70/80 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to push the dollar decisively higher," said Takashi Toyahara, manager of the fixed-income section at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  "Having broken through key resistance levels after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, the market could now test the dollar towards 119.20-119.30 yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Japan is battling to restrain the yen in order to prevent deflationary pressure from worsening and to help domestic manufacturers. \par \par \uc2  Thursday saw \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third day this week. On Wednesday the Japanese monetary authorities sold yen and enlisted the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) to act on their behalf to buy euros for yen in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came at a time when many traders were off for their lunch break in Tokyo, which exaggerated the upward swing. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed that the BOJ had bought dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had backed off its highs after profit-taking and selling from exporters, but the market was apparently reluctant to go short of dollars following recent aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro fell to $0.9181/86 against $0.9232/37. Against the yen, it stood at 108.92/9.02 yen against 108.61/72 yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CAUTION \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar was well-supported since early trade, with many dealers placing bids around 117.50 yen on expectations that the BOJ would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  today. \par \par \uc2  "Yesterday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the BOJ stepped in to buy dollars from around 117.40 yen surprised the market, so many dealers had prepared for more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  today," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Despite the BOJ's effort, general sentiment on the dollar remained frail with the Dow Jones industrial average falling 1.1 percent on Wednesday as warnings about corporate earnings snapped a two-day relief rally. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday that U.S. economic growth was skidding lower before the September 11 attacks and now it was impossible to predict what would happen. \par \par \uc2  Under these circumstances, the U.S. Federal Reserve was expected to cut its Federal funds rate by 50 basis points at next week's policy-setting meeting. \par \par \uc2  Many cash-rich Japanese institutional investors were trying to make decisions on what they should do with their money in the second half of the fiscal year, with some looking for an opportunity to buy U.S. dollar-denominated bonds, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said investors were showing interest in buying U.S. bonds of maturities between two and five years, but looking for more clues on the outlook of the U.S. economy and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  A bond trader at a Japanese bank said that investors were also looking at U.S. corporate bonds with credit ratings above A grade with spreads more than 50 basis points above Treasuries. \par \par \uc2  SWISS FRANC \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc, a traditional safe haven for worried investors, continued to lose its recent lustre despite jitters over imminent U.S. military action, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have noted the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's desire to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively, lowering interest rates by a full percentage point in September as it soared on safe-haven flows. \par \par \uc2  The dollar advanced sharply to 1.6063/69 francs from 1.5990 in late offshore on Wednesday and more than three centimes better than Friday's 20-month lows. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that the Swiss franc should remain well-supported as long as the possibility of U.S. retaliation for the attacks looked near. \par \par \uc2  Rising at the expense of the Swiss franc was the British pound, which climbed to near eight-month highs against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Helped by investors switching into higher-yielding assets, sterling held overnight gains at $1.4750. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0608 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2000 Cls\par                                 26 Sep.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr    