\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL ECONOMY-Policy-makers battle gloomy global outlook. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Knut Engelmann \par \uc2 1,191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 04:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Policy-makers from the world's leading industrial nations battled on Wednesday to counter growing pessimism over the global economic outlook as the International Monetary Fund warned of feeble growth ahead. \par \par \uc2  The world economy was already slowing before the Sept. 11 hijacked jetliner attacks in the United States and now faces an even worse prognosis, the Washington-based international lender said in its flagship World Economic Outlook. \par \par \uc2  Worries over a prolonged downturn in the world's biggest economy, already hurt by a plunge in U.S. consumer confidence in the wake of the terror strikes, and a flood of gloomy corporate news weighed on Wall Street, where stocks fell. \par \par \uc2  In Germany, Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder joined a cluster of European officials who insisted the attacks would fail to undermine world economic growth. He also urged central bankers and policy-makers to continue coordinated action. \par \par \uc2  "Terrorists cannot win against the world economy because the basis for growth and well-being in our country, in Europe and abroad is intact. There is no reason for pessimism," Schroeder told the parliament in the Berlin. \par \par \uc2  But ratings agency Standard & Poor's said the attacks in New York and near Washington would push the United States into recession in the latter half of this year and grind global economic growth to a near-standstill. \par \par \uc2  "The terrorist attacks will hurt U.S. consumer confidence and will likely push the country into a recession for at least the third and fourth quarters of 2001, with the global economy at near zero growth," S&P said. The IMF forecast global economic growth of just 2.6 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  The new forecast factors out the attacks, which were too recent to be fully analyzed. But it was already sharply lower than an April estimate of 3.2 percent and well below the 4.7 percent global expansion the world enjoyed last year. \par \par \uc2  The fund said its forecast reflected a delayed U.S. recovery, a sharp European downturn and a Japanese economy on the brink of recession. \par \par \uc2  "Clearly, recent events will have an impact on activity in the short term and add to the already significant downside risks both in the United States and elsewhere," it said. \par \par \uc2  CRUSHING BLOW \par \par \uc2  The attacks left almost 7,000 dead or missing and dealt a crushing economic blow, causing the worst drop in U.S. stocks since the Great Depression of the 1930s and eroding the consumer confidence that had kept the world's richest economy afloat. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill repeated that the country's battered economy should bounce back. "My own view is the recovery has been slowed by a quarter or so," he told a group of industry executives meeting in New York. \par \par \uc2  He also cautioned that the government should not rush into new stimulus measures that could create complications for the economy further down the road, echoing similar remarks by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan over recent days. \par \par \uc2  "We must move carefully and deliberately in a way so we don't have regrets in a year from now," O'Neill said. "It's important we don't do too much or too permanently and have effects on the economy that are not desirable." \par \par \uc2  Lawmakers are mulling new stimulus plans to give the ailing U.S. economy a shot in the arm and boost battered consumer spirits. U.S. airlines alone stand to get $15 billion in emergency funds to help them get through a deep slump in the wake of the attacks, in which hijacked airliners were crashed into the World Trade Center and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  Congress has already passed a $40 billion emergency relief package and economists expect total new government measures to reach $100 billion. \par \par \uc2  Both Greenspan and O'Neill have urged lawmakers to wait until the full economic impact of the strikes becomes a little clearer before committing to large-scale spending plans that risk firing up inflation and could make it more difficult for the Fed to maintain its own strategy of relaxing monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already cut key interest rates eight times this year and is expected to do so again when policy-makers next meet on Oct. 2. \par \par \uc2  FLOOD OF GLOOMY NEWS \par \par \uc2  Amid a flood of weak earnings news and profit warnings, equity markets remained jittery. The Dow Jones industrial average fell 1.1 percent. European stock markets made small gains, but struggled to push much higher as fears of U.S.-led military retaliation against those thought to be responsible for the suicide attacks loomed large. \par \par \uc2  In London, the world's biggest insurance market, Lloyd's of London, said its total losses from the attacks would amount to a whopping 1.3 billion pounds ($1.92 billion). \par \par \uc2  Top Wall Street firms, such as Goldman Sachs and Bear Stearns, posted weak quarterly earnings and said the attacks that struck the heart of the U.S. financial center would make for difficult conditions ahead. \par \par \uc2  In Switzerland, banking group Credit Suisse warned that it expected operating results in the third quarter to be well below those in the April-June period. \par \par \uc2  In the hard-hit airline industry, Delta, the No. 3 U.S. carrier, said it would slash 13,000 jobs and cut its flight schedule by 15 percent, adding to the tens of thousands of airline job losses announced after the Sept. 11 attacks caused a plunge in air travel and rising security costs. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling the yen to stem an export-crimping rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dutch confidence figures released Wednesday showed consumers reeling after the deadly attacks on the United States two weeks ago, reinforcing recent economic data that have encouraged fears that a recession was on its way. \par \par \uc2  German retail association HDE reiterated that it expected retail sales to fall by 0.5 percent in real terms this year, partly due to the economic impact of the terror strikes. \par \par \uc2  "So far we have seen no significant change in the behavior of the consumer," HDE President Hermann Franzen said. "But the risks have increased. The mood is subdued." \par \par \uc2  The European data came a day after fresh U.S. figures on Tuesday showed a plunge in consumer confidence that heightened doubts over a speedy U.S. recovery. U.S. confidence suffered its sharpest monthly drop in September in almost 11 years. \par \par \uc2  However, 88 percent of the survey responses were recorded before the Sept. 11 attacks, which led many economists to conclude that confidence would take an even harder hit once those effects were measured in the October survey. \par \par \uc2  In Germany, Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke was quoted as saying the European economy stood a chance of picking up in the fourth quarter and that the deadly attacks on U.S. targets should not shake the foundations of the global economy. \par \par \uc2  "The attacks are not a warning signal for the world economy," he told Handelsblatt daily. (Additional reporting by Mark Egan in Washington, Andrew Priest in New York, and Clifford Coonan in Berlin). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r00465\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
