\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks fall, bonds surge on economy worries. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Atiya Hussain \par \uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2001\par 04:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 26 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks fell on Wednesday on renewed signs of corporate malaise while U.S. Treasuries shot higher as uncertainty over U.S. military plans after the Sept. 11 attacks compounded fears of a possible global recession. \par \par \uc2  Bonds and gold rose, reflecting persistent investor demand for the safest of assets in the two weeks that followed the attacks that destroyed the twin towers of New York's World Trade Center and damaged the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  Volatile oil prices hit nearly two-year lows on fears a worldwide recession will sap energy demand, but ended higher as bargain hunters emerged after a nearly $8 slump, or about 25 percent, in crude oil prices in less than two weeks. \par \par \uc2  Warnings about corporate earnings weighed heavily on Wall Street, snapping a two-day relief rally that followed the stock market's worst week since the Great Depression. \par \par \uc2  Fragile sentiment in the wake of the attacks that left about 7,000 people missing or dead was further bruised as analysts cut their earnings forecasts for industry leaders International Business Machines Corp. and Caterpillar Inc. and top Wall Street firms, such as Goldman Sachs and Bear Stearns, posted weak quarterly earnings. \par \par \uc2  "We'll see a whole month of bad news ahead," said Chuck Hill, director of research at earnings tracking firm Thomson Financial/First Call. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Dow Jones industrial average closed down 92.58 points, or 1.07 percent, at 8,567.39. The technology-packed Nasdaq Composite Index fell 37.60 points, or 2.50 percent, to 1,464.04. The broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index was down 5.23 points, or 0.52 percent, at 1,007.04. \par \par \uc2  The rally in U.S. Treasuries drove yields on five-year notes close to their lowest levels since May 1963. Yields on short-term two-year notes also fell, matching their lowest levels since the securities were first sold in 1972. \par \par \uc2  The falling short-term yields reflect bets the U.S. Federal Reserve will aggressively lower interest rates when it meets next Tuesday to try to ward off recession. \par \par \uc2  SAFE HAVEN GOLD UP, BUT SWISS FRANC GIVES BACK GROUND \par \par \uc2  Gold, another beneficiary of global investors' risk aversion, was lifted on Wednesday. Gold prices have soared $20, or 7.7 percent, since the attacks and on Wednesday spot gold closed at $293.00/293.80 per troy ounce, up from Tuesday's $290.10/291.10. \par \par \uc2  But the Swiss franc, also a traditional safe haven for worried investors, lost some of its recent strong gains, giving up a full percent against the dollar. The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , worried by the franc's strength, has aggressively lowered interest rates by a full percentage point in September to try to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded around 1.5990 francs, more than 3 centimes above last Friday's 20-month lows. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also firmer against a broadly weaker yen, which fell after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday - the fifth time in the past week and a half - to cap the yen's export-crimping strength. The BOJ enlisted the help the of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, as it had on Monday, giving the euro a boost against the yen as well. \par \par \uc2  The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries oil cartel, meeting in Vienna on Wednesday, ruled out production cuts that might have hurt the fragile global economic growth by raising the cost of energy supplies. \par \par \uc2  New York crude oil futures fell below $21 for the first time since October 1999 shortly after trading began on the New York Mercantile Exchange, but closed more than 0.50 cents higher at $22.38 per barrel. \par \par \uc2  U.S. STOCKS FAIL TO MATCH EUROPEAN GAINS \par \par \uc2  While Wall Street sagged, European stocks rose on Wednesday. The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 index closed 1.31 percent and the DJ Euro Stoxx 50 ended nearly 1.94 percent stronger. \par \par \uc2  "Overseas markets had done better overnight and that got the U.S. stock market going initially. But there is still a hangover from yesterday's data showing that consumer confidence was deteriorating even before the Sept. 11 attacks," said Kevin Logan, senior market economist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in New York. \par \par \uc2  Fears of a U.S. recession were heightened on Tuesday by a plunge in U.S. consumer confidence in September that threatens to undermine retail spending, which has been one of the bright spots in the otherwise sluggish U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  U.S. ratings agency Standard & Poor's on Wednesday said the attacks will push the United States into recession in the latter half of this year and grind global economic growth to a near standstill, \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund also painted a grim picture, saying global growth was already on the brink of recession before the Sept. 11 attacks on the United States, and now faces an even worse prognosis. \par \par \uc2  The crisis-ridden global airline industry got more bad news on Wednesday, as Delta Air Lines, the No. 3 U.S. airline, became the latest major carrier to announce job cuts and reductions in flight schedules. Delta said 13,000 jobs would have to go. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9r0040o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
