\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Volatilities Up In Line With Spot Moves \b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 22:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were higher Wednesday afternoon in New York. The activity was in line with moves in the spot market and reflected more uncertainty about the direction of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar gained against the yen and euro. \par \par \uc2  Volatility has also been boosted by yet another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although the dollar's upside has mainly been to counteract dollar selling by Japanese exporters overnight. \par \par \uc2  But after the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fifth bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in eight working days, some dealers said the tide is finally turning in favor of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The first couple of times the market can take advantage of their lack of success," said Kenneth Agostino, trading manager at GAIN Capital in Warren, N.J. "But [the BOJ officials] have much deeper pockets than anyone in the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Underscoring the fact that banks are solidifying their views on dollar/yen, Citibank Wednesday initiated a yen buy against the dollar at Y117.95 and will exit at Y118.40. But technical data suggest the dollar could travel as low as Y112, said Robert Sinche, Citibank's head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy in New York. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg, meanwhile, initiated a long dollar/yen position shortly before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In a note to clients, UBS cited the folly of buying yen on a safe-haven bargain, expecting this "knee-jerk" reaction to reverse "as Japanese fundamentals start to deteriorate and the BOJ steps up its efforts to weaken the yen." \par \par \uc2  Late afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y117.74, down from Y117.85 in London but up from Y117.60 Tuesday in New York. The euro, meanwhile, was at $0.9238, up from $0.9220 in London and from $0.9226 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options imply volatility of 12.6%-13.1%, up from 11.6%-11.9% earlier in London and from 12.4%-12.9% in New York Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Volatility measures the expected range in the value of an asset over the period of the contract. Volatilities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options gauge the anticipated moves in the underlying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a higher volatility implying a wider trading range. \par \par \uc2  One-month euro/dollar option volatilities are at 13.0%-13.5% from 12.7%-13.0% earlier in London and from 11.05%-11.15%, in New York late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  One-month risk-reversals on dollar/yen favor dollar puts at 1.6%-2.3%, down from 1.75%-2.5% earlier in London and compare with 1.9%-2.5% in New York late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Risk-reversals measure the market preference for either put or call options by the premium or discount applied. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro calls are favored against the dollar by 0.8%-1.4%, from\par 1.05%-1.35% in London and  0.95%-1.45% in New York.\par     -Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4156;\par     grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1077 4127 44427 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010926dx9q001bv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png