\par\par \b \uc2  UBS Warburg Recommends Long USD/JPY Position \b0\par\par\uc2 470 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 17:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Strategists at UBS Warburg recommended establishing a long dollar/yen position shortly before the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which boosted the dollar in the process. \par \par \uc2  In a note to clients, UBS cited the folly of buying yen on a safe-haven bargain, expecting this "knee-jerk" reaction to reverse "as Japanese fundamentals start to deteriorate and the BOJ steps up its efforts to weaken the yen." \par \par \uc2  Fears that many Japanese investors are repatriating funds ahead of the mid-fiscal year end should also subside as the Sep. 30 cut-off date approaches, the note said, allowing the dollar/yen pair to head higher. \par \par \uc2  This new position is not a big departure for UBS, which has been in the yen bear camp for quite some time, said Shahab Jalinoos, a UBS strategist in London. But the bank avoided active trades until now, due to a climate of high risk aversion in the wake of the World Trade Center attacks on Sept. 11. \par \par \uc2  Above all, the BOJ is becoming more aggressive in its efforts to rein in the yen, and the results are starting to show. Wednesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market for the fifth time in two weeks, selling euros against yen and in turn lifting the dollar from an overnight low of Y117.10 to an intraday high above Y118. \par \par \uc2  "Given the fact that the Japanese authorities went out of their way to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday on a holiday and through the [European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ], it shows they're upping the ante," said Dustin Reid, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Stamford, Conn. \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japanese Governor Masaru Hayami Wednesday also supported the UBS call, Reid said. Hayami, who was in the recent past considered something of a yen bull, told reporters after a meeting of the government's Council on Economic and Fiscal Policy that "it isn't good for the dollar to fall or to have its credibility questioned, as it is an international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Independent of the UBS trading call, UBS's Japanese economics team had recently downgraded its forecasts for the country's growth, Reid said. \par \par \uc2  The bank's economists predict real annualized gross national product to register at -0.9% in 2001 and -0.8% in 2002, a figure that had previously been positive. \par \par \uc2  "Yes, it can get worse and yes, the market will start to bring back fundamentals," Reid said. \par \par \uc2  UBS opened the dollar/yen trade at Y117.55, with a target of Y125 and a stop at Y115.50. \par \par \uc2  The risk to the trade recommendation, Reid said, is the bearish tendency in dollar/yen until the pair closes above Y118.30. \par \par \uc2  -By Jennifer Downey, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2019; jennifer.downey@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010926dx9q0010h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
