\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks rise, bonds fall, watching OPEC. \b0\par\par\uc2 923 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2     In LONDON story headlined GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks rise, bonds \par \par \uc2  fall, watching OPEC please read in first para ...after the Bank \par \par \uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ... \par \par \uc2  instead of ...after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Corrects to make clear the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2  Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was acting on behalf of the Bank of Japan) \par \par \uc2  Also please read in para two ...The Bank of Japan aimed to \par \par \uc2  weaken... instead of ...The central banks aimed to weaken... \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows. \par \par \uc2  By Nigel Stephenson \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - European stocks edged up and bonds fell on Wednes day as markets eyed a meeting of OPEC oil ministers while the dollar and euro rose versus the yen after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last week hit seven-month highs against the dollar on heightened risk aversion following the September 11 attacks on U.S. cities. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices continued their fall, triggered by a conviction the world was headed for recession and that the OPEC cartel can do little to shore up prices. \par \par \uc2  OPEC ministers, beginning a conference in Vienna, were expected to leave in place supply from 10 member countries at 23.2 million barrels a day. \par \par \uc2  "The September cuts still need time to work," Saudi Oil Minister Ali al-Naimi said, referring to production cuts which came into effect at the beginning of September. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices have declined steeply for the past two weeks as recession fears in the wake of the September 11 attacks have replaced concerns U.S. retaliation could disrupt Middle East crude supplies. \par \par \uc2  European bourses dipped at the opening after muted gains on Wall Street, where the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed 0.65 percent higher, with technology and oil stocks leading the decline. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 index , which rose 1.2 percent on Tuesday, was 0.55 percent higher and the narrower blue-chip DJ Euro Stoxx 50 was up 0.41 percent. \par \par \uc2  TECHNOLOGY SHARES UNDER PRESSURE \par \par \uc2  Technology was the weakest sector in the DJ index . Finnish mobile phone giant Nokia was down more than four percent. STMicroelectronics was down 1.75 percent and ASML down nearky four percent. \par \par \uc2  Among oil stocks, Shell was off 1.07 percent, though Royal Dutch and BP were both higher. \par \par \uc2  The hesitant start in Europe followed a 0.54 percent fall in Tokyo's Nikkei average as banks fell and globally-exposed companies took a hit from Tuesday's data showing the sharpest drop in U.S. consumer confidence in almost 11 years. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Conference Board's confidence index for September, based on a survey taken either side of the September 11 attacks on New York and Washington, slid to 97.6 from 114 in August. \par \par \uc2  The gloomy data weighed on U.S. stocks but upbeat forecasts from plane-maker Boeing and others offset worries about the costs of the U.S. military campaign and the economy. \par \par \uc2  However, both futures on both the S&P 500 index and the Nasdaq were down in early trade, indicating a weaker start on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  BOND YIELDS RISE \par \par \uc2  Short-dated government bond yields, which have slid since September 11 as investors have sought a safe haven from falling stock markets, moved higher as stocks were relatively stable. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said diminished concerns over the scale of U.S. military action had pushed bond prices down. \par \par \uc2  As the U.S military build-up within range of Afghanistan continued, Defense Secretary Donald Rumsfeld said the United States would not launch a massive D-Day invasion, but was preparing for a long, deadly fight. \par \par \uc2  "Safe-haven trades are easing off because the risksof all out war do not seem as great as they were in the last couple of weeks," said Aongus Buckley, economist at GNI. "Consolidation is the best bet unless stock market sell off sharply." \par \par \uc2  At 0830 GMT, the two-year Schatz was yielding 3.484 percent, up 2.5 basis points but still close to the two-year lows hit after the attacks. The 10-year Bund yield was up 0.1 basis points at 4.841 percent. \par \par \uc2  Among corporate bonds, France Telecom euro issues underperformed after rating s agency Standard & Poor's cut its long-term rating to BBB+ from A, citing the company's revised debt reduction targets. \par \par \uc2  France Telecom's 6.125 percent euro bond due November 2005 was bid at 210 basis points over government bonds on Wednesday, 10 basis points wider than on Tuesday, dealers said. The company's 6.625 percent euro bonds due November 2010 were 13 wider at 235 over government bonds. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at 117.90 yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese Finance Ministry official said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had taken "appropriate action" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Under the instruction of Finanec Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, we have juts taken appropriate action in the foriegn exchange market," the head of the ministry's international bureau Zembei Mizoguchi told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The ECB confimed it bought euros against the yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The euro was around $0.9216 and 108.68 yen . \par \par \uc2  Gold , traditionally viewed as a safe haven in times of turmoil, rose in early European trade to $291.50/2.50 an ounce, compared with the New York close at $290.10/1.10. \par \par \uc2  -additional reporting by Sabrina Ghani, Alex Clelland, Emily Kaiser and Dhara Ranasinghe. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011001dx9q017ov\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
