\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Japan pushes yen down, ECB acts as its agent. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sumeet Desai \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 12:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The yen fell against the euro and the dollar on Wednesday after Japan once more stepped into the markets to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , enlisting the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its agent for the second time this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar and the euro jumped half a yen as Japan and the ECB confirmed they had been selling the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been strengthening recently as investors repatriate assets ahead of the fiscal half-year end and as risk aversion grows. \par \par \uc2  Japan began its current campaign to depress the yen more than a week ago, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least five separate days, across all time zones and twice with the assistance of the ECB. \par \par \uc2  "The yen weakened. We've had confirmation from the BOJ and the ECB acting on its behalf," said Michael Metcalfe, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eocnomist at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "But they're going to have to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as long as these repatriation flows continue." \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose as high as 117.97 yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before easing back to around 117.80 by 1030 GMT. The the euro rose to around 108.90 yen before slipping to 108.65. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the euro was steady against the dollar around $0.9225. \par \par \uc2  REPATRIATION MIGHT BE DRYING UP \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Japan might find it easier to restrain upward pressure on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the close of business for the day as Wednesday is the last \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading day ahead of the interim fiscal book-closing on Friday. \par \par \uc2  As a rule, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  settlement takes place two business days after a transaction. \par \par \uc2  "Depending on how much of the repatriation flows are due to half-year end or to safe-haven flows, there's a possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might become easier," said Metcalfe. \par \par \uc2  Otherwise, dealers said concern about possible coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Group of Seven was allayed after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister Masajuro Shiokawa said finance ministers from the seven richest countries did not discuss this in a teleconference on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  SWISSIE LOSES GROUND \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc lost nearly one percent against both the euro and the dollar as dealers noted the Swiss National Bank's determination to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has soared in recent days on safe-haven flows. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should remain fairly well-supported as long as the threat of U.S. retaliation for the attacks on its cities hung in the air. \par \par \uc2  "Continued global risk aversion means the Swiss franc is likely to remain generally well bid," said Kamal Sharma, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Commerzbank. \par \par \uc2  Besides \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wariness, traders said international events surrounding the possibility of conflict in Afghanistan was dominating talk in dealing rooms. \par \par \uc2  And with little major economic news on the agenda, attention was turning to Friday's University of Michigan consumer sentiment index for further clues on the impact of the airplane attacks on U.S. consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the U.S. Conference Board said its monthly index of consumer confidence dropped 16.4 points to 97.6 in September from a month earlier, much lower than analysts' forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The survey reflected only part of the shock of the attacks, which bodes ill for retail spending which had been propping up the sluggish U.S. economy before the strikes. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is fragile and the consequences are the dollar will struggle, with the U.S. consumer confidence data suggesting the U.S. is in or on the edge of recession,." said Nick Stamenkovic, senior strategist at Nomura International. \par \par \uc2  GLOOMY OUTLOOK \par \par \uc2  U.S. credit rating company Standard & Poor's said on Wednesday that it saw the September 11 attacks pushing the United States into recession in the third and fourth quarters. \par \par \uc2  It also said chances were increasing of a sharper downgrade of Japan's sovereign credit rating within the AA category, signalling that it may cut the rating more than a notch from its current double A-plus (AA+). \par \par \uc2  The rating company put Japan on review for possible downgrade earlier this month. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, meanwhile, was quoted as saying that the European economy stood a good chance of picking up in the fourth quarter and the attacks on the United States should not shake the foundations of the global economy. \par \par \uc2  Welteke, also a member of the ECB governing council, told Germany's Handelsblatt daily that the ECB's half-point rate cut last week aimed to boost confidence after the assaults on two U.S. cities. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010927dx9q00e7p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
