\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Confidence shaken along with U.S. consumers. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Chen \par \uc2 983 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 15:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Asian equities markets were conflicted on Wednesday with investors glum about prospects that the region's exports would plunge along with the confidence of U.S. consumers. \par \par \uc2  And while the dollar was despondent over news Tuesday that the U.S. Conference Board's consumer confidence index fell to an 11-year low in September, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little to cheer about as the United States is Asia's biggest export market. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares slipped, with banking shares dragging the benchmark Nikkei average lower as investors worried over the impact of bad loans on the balance sheets of local banks. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over the struggling Japanese economy prompted ratings agency Standard & Poor's to warn it could cut the country's sovereign rating from its current AA plus. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng rose thanks to a technical rebound in HSBC Holdings. But volumes were thin and many players stuck to the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  Korea and Taiwan's stock markets were given a helping hand by their governments, directly and indirectly. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Seoul edged higher as the government's plans to increase spending on infrastructure and public works projects pulled up construction and machinery makers' shares. \par \par \uc2  Buying by government-linked funds pulled Taiwan stocks firmly into postivie territory. \par \par \uc2  REGIONAL \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  WORRIES WITH DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  While U.S. stocks resisted the temptation to despair over falling consumer confidence, the dollar suffered at the hands of European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to claw back ground from the euro, but steadied against the yen, as the market remained wary over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the market to buy dollars to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export price competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Recent gains in the yen did little to brighten the prospect for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have long been focused on growth prospects closely tied to U.S. fortunes. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar weakening against the yen has not helped Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," JP Morgan Chase said in a recent report. "Weaker growth prospects and oil supply uncertainties are negative for most floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - except for the Indonesian rupiah." \par \par \uc2  "Even more negative for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the rise in risk aversion," it added. \par \par \uc2  While the Singapore dollar backed away from six-week lows of around 1.7752 seen Tuesday, dealers said sentiment had turned against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Indeed, the local dollar has lost some 2 percent of its value since September 17, when it hit a nine-month high of around 1.7280. \par \par \uc2  NO THREAT TO RATINGS ... YET \par \par \uc2  Standard & Poor's said in a report the attacks in New York and Washington would push the U.S. economy into recession this year and send global economic growth to a near standstill. \par \par \uc2  But the ratings agency said it did not see major changes to the sovereign ratings of the 17 Asian nations it covers, though it delivered warnings about Japan and the more open economies of Southeast Asia, such as Malaysia and Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The report also said while Asian central banks did have room to loosen monetary policy further to bolster growth, some countries could be hamstrung due to expensive bank bail-outs. \par \par \uc2  Both Malaysia and Singapore announced steps pump-prime their economies, given the sharp downturn in external demand. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia unveiled a 4.6 billion ringgit ($1.1 billion) stimulus plan. That follows earlier spending packages this year of 28 billion ringgit and 3 billion ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Singapore said it would introduce off-budget measures to boost the economy, already in a technical recession, in mid-October. It also said the package would likely be bigger than the S$2.2 billion ($1.26 billion) plan announced in July. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts were sceptical. \par \par \uc2  "These are externally led economies, and external demand accounts for something like 80 percent of total demand," Song Sen Wun, Vice President of Regional Economics at GK Goh in Singapore, told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  "So, you, at best, can just dampen the slide somewhat. You can't turn it around." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. $0721 GMT Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Japan yen     117.30       117.01      -0.25\par   Sing dlr      1.7693       1.7673      -0.11\par   Taiwan dlr    34.573       34.574      +0.00\par   Korean won   1302.00      1297.00      -0.38\par   Baht           44.39        44.42      +0.07\par   Peso           51.30        51.24      -0.12\par   Rupiah       9620.00      9500.00      -1.25\par   Ringgit       3.7994       3.7995      +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par   Japan yen     117.3