\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Japan acts again to halt yen's rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 12:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 26 (Reuters) - Japan acted again on Wednesday to stop a painful rise in the yen, buying dollars and euros for the yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "We are doing this with a firm attitude," Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told reporters as he left the ministry, adding that the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying euros for yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan. The ECB also confirmed the move. \par \par \uc2  Mizoguchi added that Japan was buying dollars for yen in European trading time, but declined to comment on how it was conducting the dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tokyo launched a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign last week to stem a rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid fears a strong yen will hit exports in another blow to the recession-bound economy. \par \par \uc2  As confirmation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit the market, the yen fell as far as 117.97 per dollar . Against the euro , it fell to the day's lows around 108.87 yen. \par \par \uc2  At 0950 GMT, the dollar was hovering near 117.90 yen, while the euro was standing at around 108.60 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Under the instruction of Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, we have just taken appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Mizoguchi, the head of the ministry's international bureau, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "We will continue to monitor the market carefully and take appropriate action, if needed, for the stability of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement," he said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, Shiokawa told a news conference that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not asked for concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, adding that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone. \par \par \uc2  Asked whether Group of Seven finance ministers discussed the issue of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during a conference call on Tuesday, Shiokawa said: "No, we didn't. So far, we have not requested it and we have not received any offers." \par \par \uc2  He added that he was not sure at the moment whether he would ask for G7 cooperation on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding that a G7 finance ministers' meeting was still far away. \par \par \uc2  Finance ministers and central bankers from the key industrial nations will meet on October 6 in Washington to discuss global prospects after the devastating attacks in the United States on September 11 cast a cloud over the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dx9q052kl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
