\par\par \b \uc2  Yen falls across board as BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2001\par 11:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 26 (Reuters) - The yen fell nearly half a percent against the dollar and even more against the euro on Wednesday as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had taken "appropriate action" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets while the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it had bought euros for yen on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ, on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has sold yen for dollars several times over the past week on worries a strengthening yen will dent exporters' competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The latest bout of yen selling followed comments earlier in the day by BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami who said it was not good for the dollar to fall. \par \par \uc2  The yen fell as far as 117.97 per dollar , according to Reuters data. Against the euro, it fell to the day's lows around 108.87 yen . \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit seven-month highs against the dollar last week, buoyed by heightened risk aversion in the wake of the attacks on the United States a fortnight ago. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dx9q04f9o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
