\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ seen refusing to blink in yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Javier David \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2001\par 22:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 25 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets from driving the yen higher appear to be working in the short-term, but it remains to be seen whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can hold the yen back indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen as much as 5 percent against the dollar since the Sept. 11 terror attacks on the United States made investors increasingly reluctant to hold the once-cherished greenback. \par \par \uc2  After multiple warnings, the Bank of Japan - acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has waded into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on four separate occasions in the past week to weaken the yen at a time when Japan's exporters and stagnant economy can ill-afford the luxury of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the dollar bought 117.60 yen in late U.S. trading, up from a seven-month low of 115.76 hit last week. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say Japan has drawn a line in the sand around the 117 yen level to keep the dollar from falling to lower levels, which will test the pain threshold for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export sector. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have made the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market acutely sensitive to the prospect that monetary authorities could be lying in wait to punish traders looking to take the yen higher. \par \par \uc2  According to analysts, that has altered the market's psychology. \par \par \uc2  "Once the BOJ goes into \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  mode, it makes traders think twice before taking any positions," said Guillermo Estebanez, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  PERSISTENT BOJ HAVING SHORT-TERM SUCCESS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are often doomed to failure, as central banks pit their reserves against far larger flows of capital and speculators in the more than $1 trillion a day \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of Japan appears to be having success, at least temporarily. \par \par \uc2  It has acted during Asian, European and North American trading sessions, and on one occasion enlisted the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its effort. \par \par \uc2  An unusual aspect of the recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say, is that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen selling was what is known in the market as "unsterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , meaning the BOJ leaves money in the financial system. In "sterilized" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would absorb all the funds it injects to buy a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , usually via a sale of government debt. \par \par \uc2  Unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "may be more effective because when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is defending a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is normally defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against weakness, and risks running out of reserves," said Chris Widness, international economist at J.P. Morgan Chase. \par \par \uc2  "But when it is (dampening) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, you can create all the supply you need," by selling yen against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America's Estebanez agreed, saying that, in theory, the excess liquidity was likely to temper demand for yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "Definitely theory tells you that not sterilizing is going to have much more of an impact," he said. \par \par \uc2  But Estebanez said it was far from clear whether Japan could hold the yen back indefinitely. \par \par \uc2  The September 30 fiscal half-year-end book closing in Japan has amplified the yen's strength, as Japanese firms repatriate capital invested abroad to cover domestic losses. \par \par \uc2  This is not lost on Japanese policy-makers, who may be trying simply to ride out a temporary storm, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is not in fact concerned that we will stay above 117 indefinitely, but they really want to make it somewhat costly for traders to push below the 117 level," said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Brown Brothers Harriman. \par \par \uc2  "What they really want to do is avoid changing (trading) behavior back to the 115-120 trading band," preferring to see the market remain within a 120-125 range, she said. \par \par \uc2  Beyond the issue of fiscal half-year flows, the Bank of Japan may be powerless should \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets continue to sell the dollar due to a bleak outlook in the United States. \par \par \uc2  "The main piece of news will be where the U.S. is going ... and how the market eventually assesses recovery chances in the U.S.," said Estebanez. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925dx9p07yvx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
