\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia bounces on Wall St rise, investors wary. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Valerie Lee \par \uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2001\par 08:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 25 (Reuters) - Downtrodden Asian bourses rose tentatively across the region on Tuesday, boosted by Wall Street's strength, while the dollar hung onto its offshore gains in early Tokyo trade. \par \par \uc2  Shares on Wall Street snapped a week of brutal selling on Monday as the Dow Jones Industrial Average racked up its fifth-biggest point-gain ever, rising 368.05 points or 4.47 percent to 8,603.86. The tech-heavy Nasdaq composite index rose 5.35 percent to 1,499.40. \par \par \uc2  Despite the rises in most Asian markets, investors and analysts reamined wary, warning the bounce could be short-lived as global and local economic fundamentals remained gloomy and worries of impending war persist. \par \par \uc2  Sure enough, Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei share average halved its early gain of more than three percent and Hong Kong's benchmark Hang Seng index fell into negative territory after rising two percent soon after the opening. \par \par \uc2  "People are still very cautious about what is going to happen over in American markets. We are obviously going to be kicked around a bit by America," said Wren Bligh, a private client adviser at Australian brokers Wilson HTM. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a lot of blue chips, a lot have been sold down a long way, (and) people are jumping into those. Whether or not it is sustainable in the current market we shall see," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, dealers said the dollar, while vulnerable, held its ground on Tuesday around 117.35 yen due to fears of renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) has been a heavy seller of yen in the past week to try to head off a damaging rise in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it was not seen in the market on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  U.S. ECONOMY STRONGER? \par \par \uc2  Shares in the United States drew strength from President George W. Bush's confident comments that the U.S. economy remains fundamentally sound. \par \par \uc2  Most economists think the $10 trillion U.S. economy, which was already grinding to a standstill before the September 11 strikes, is firmly in the grip of recession. Few expect it now to bounce back before next year. \par \par \uc2  But expectations of further monetary easing - the U.S. Federal Reserve led a coordinated round of global rate cuts last week - coupled with tens of billions of dollars of extra spending planned by the U.S. government could make the subsequent economic recovery sharper than it would have been. \par \par \uc2  Still, Asian investors were not raising their hopes too high despite Tuesday's gains. \par \par \uc2  "We advise investors against rushing into the market," Singapore's Kim Eng Securities said in a note to investors. "Be wary that this might just be a case of a dead cat bouncing." \par \par \uc2  CRUDE FUTURES RECOVER \par \par \uc2  In the oil markets, NYMEX crude oil futures edged up in electronic ACCESS trading on Tuesday, following a sixth straight day of losses during daytime trading in New York triggered by fears of global recession. \par \par \uc2  A broker said NYMEX crude rebounded on a technical correction after the steep fall, but sentiment remained bearish. \par \par \uc2  Traders said confidence in the market has evaporated as fears that U.S. reprisals for the September 11 attacks could disrupt oil supplies gave way to increasing gloom over fuel demand prospects if the world economy slithers into recession. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925dx9p02z9a\par }\page {\par\fs2