\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Gains in Trade With Yen After Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2001\par 00:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar made no headway against the euro Monday but firmed against the yen, supported by another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and boosted by a 350-point rally in the Dow Jones Industrial Average. \par \par \uc2  In late trading in New York, the euro was changing hands at 91.55 cents, above 91.46 cents earlier in London and above 91.37 cents in New York on Friday. Against the yen, the dollar was quoted at 117.52 yen, up from 116.37 yen early in Europe and 116.19 yen late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also quoted at 107.65 yen, up sharply from 106.19 yen in New York late Friday. The pound was trading at 1.4618 up from 1.4532 late Friday in New York and the dollar was at 1.5985 Swiss francs compared with 1.5831. \par \par \uc2  In New York, the dollar's more positive tone was little impacted by a Conference Board announcement that the composite index of leading indicators fell 0.3% in August to 109.6, marking the first time in five months the index had declined. \par \par \uc2  After its action in Tokyo, a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official confirmed that the authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying dollars for yen, reinforcing the notion that Japanese public holidays are no barrier to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Later in the day, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on behalf of the BOJ, buying euros against yen. \par \par \uc2  According to ECB spokesman Manfred Koerber, the ECB was acting within a standing agency agreement between the central banks. \par \par \uc2  "We are just the agent acting for [the BOJ] on their behalf and on their account," said Mr. Koerber, who declined to specify the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or at what levels it had taken place. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ had already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on three days last week -- and several times on Friday alone -- in a bid to weaken the surging yen, which the Japanese government argues is weighing on the country's moribund economy as it seeks to avoid tumbling into its fourth recession in a decade. \par \par \uc2  U.S. analysts agree that the strength of the yen threatens the long-term health of the Japanese economy. \par \par \uc2  "If they can't do something about the [strong] yen, I'm not sure the Japanese economy can tolerate reform," said Larry Greenberg, international economist at Ried, Thunberg in Westport, Conn. \par \par \uc2  "There's going to have to be a [\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market] shakeout and the yen should weaken and the euro should strengthen," Mr. Greenberg added. \par \par \uc2  However, market watchers now say that the BOJ's persistence is having a psychological impact in changing investors' mindset towards the yen. \par \par \uc2  While she expects the euro to keep on gaining against the dollar, Susan Stearns, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Montreal in New York believes that the yen may now lose ground. "Dollar/yen can go higher to 120-125 yen," she commented. \par \par \uc2  With the euro holding to a narrow trading range Monday, the Swiss franc fell about 1% after the Swiss National Bank cut its interest rates for the second time in a week, in a bid to weaken the franc. The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered the target range for three-month Libor 0.50 percentage point to 1.75%-2.75%, with immediate effect. \par \par \uc2  "This step has been taken in reaction to the marked and rapid appreciation of the Swiss franc vis-a-vis the euro witnessed in the past few days," the SNB said. \par \par \uc2  In the past few days, the Swiss franc has been a major beneficiary of the rush to find a safe haven in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the aftermath of the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center and Pentagon on September 11. \par \par \uc2  Analysts note that the franc's rally was beginning to run out of steam as panic recedes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. But, they suggest, the Swiss franc could easily benefit from any further political and military uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real bounced back at the open Monday after hitting fresh record lows Friday, aided by rising European stocks and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move late Friday to sell dollar-indexed debt after the market close. \par \par \uc2  The real opened at 2.795 reals to the dollar, 1.4%, stronger than Friday's record closing low of 2.835 reals. In late trading, the real was quoted at 2.720 reals to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Write to John Hardy at john.hardy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020010924dx9o0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
