\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL ECONOMY-Govts struggle to adapt to new economic reality. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gerrard Raven \par \uc2 1,050 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2001\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Western governments struggled again on Monday to adapt their economies to the reality of a world on the brink of conflict in which economic growth looks set to be hesitant and feeble at best. \par \par \uc2  Britain adjusted rules on how insurers invest their money in an attempt to prevent further falls on its stock market and Switzerland cut its overnight interest rates further to attempt to stem a flood of money into its safe-haven franc. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on the foreign exchanges to prevent a further appreciation of the yen against the dollar, which could only make life more difficult for exporters in an economy where domestic growth is at a halt. \par \par \uc2  The moves came after European Union finance ministers, meeting in Liege, Belgium at the weekend, struck a deal to keep their airlines flying despite soaring insurance costs in the wake of the September 11 suicide attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  As the United States went on with its troop build-up in the Middle East and central Asia, there was further sobering news on economic growth. \par \par \uc2  EU statistics bureau Eurostat reported a 1.4 percent fall in euro zone industrial production in July and Germany's top exporters group BGA halved its forecast for growth in world trade this year. \par \par \uc2  "The recent events have increased the uncertainty and downside risks for global growth prospects," European Economic and Montary Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes said in a speech in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  The EU industrial output figure did come after a 0.9 percent rise in June, but was still far worse than analysts' expectations for a 0.8 percent dip, and left output 1.1 percent below the year ago figure. The German wholesalers' and foreign trade association (BGA) cut its forecast for the global rise in exports and imports to six percent from an earlier 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  Its president, Anton F. Boerner, said the attacks on New York and Washington were likely to delay a U.S. economic upturn by one or two quarters. \par \par \uc2  "The decisive size of economic development in the United States depends in the coming months on consumer confidence. A temporary hesitancy to buy seems likely," he told a Berlin news conference. \par \par \uc2  The BGA cut its German growth forecast for this year to 1.2 percent from 1.3 percent and Didier Reynders, the Belgian finance minister who chaired the weekend meeting of his EU colleagues, told French radio the attacks on the United States would push euro zone growth down below two percent this year. \par \par \uc2  UK INSURANCE RULES ADJUSTED \par \par \uc2  In Britain, where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sir Edward George added to economic gloom at the weekend by saying he could not rule out a recession, the authorities acted quickly to ward off a stock market meltdown which could bring one closer. \par \par \uc2  The Financial Services Authority relaxed rules which forced insurers to offload shares when the market fell sharply and switch into less risky bonds. \par \par \uc2  "In current unusual market conditions, further steps are necessary to avert the need for some insurance companies to sell equities for short-term technical reasons in a way which would be damaging to the interests of their policyholders," the industry watchdog said. \par \par \uc2  The move helped British shares to stage a welcome rally, a relief after the sharp falls on every trading day last week. \par \par \uc2  Across Europe shares were up 2.5 percent by 0945 GMT on relief that the balloon had at least not gone up on U.S. military action in Afghanistan in the last couple of days. \par \par \uc2  "The word is, the world didn't end this weekend, so we're going up. It's as simple as that," said Tom Hougaard, a equities trader and analyst at Financial Spreads in London. \par \par \uc2  SWITZERLAND, JAPAN RESTRAIN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Swiss National Bank again acted to counter the strength of the country's franc, traditionally a safe haven in times of international conflict because of the country's long-standing neutrality. \par \par \uc2  It set its overnight repurchase rates at an aggressively low 2.25 percent, compared with the 2.63 percent it was prepared to pay banks for parking their money with it on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan took advantage of thin markets because of a local holiday in Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, pushing the yen down half a cent against the dollar and helping to protect its struggling export sector. \par \par \uc2  With the yen at just over 117 to the dollar at 1025 GMT, dealers agreed the Bank of Japan looked determined to defend the 115 yen level, where many Japanese companies set their internal rates. \par \par \uc2  "With stock markets falling, exporters are having difficulty. If the dollar goes lower than 115, that means most of them will be in real trouble," a European bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Another enfeebled area of the global economy, the airlines, got a further boost at the weekend from the Liege meeting of EU finance ministers. \par \par \uc2  They approved a common code which will allow the bloc's 15 member states to provide liability insurance to airlines in response to a decision by underwriters to cancel existing war risk cover on Monday. \par \par \uc2  However, the move falls well short of the bill which U.S. President George W. Bush signed on Saturday providing $15 billion for his country's struggling airlines. \par \par \uc2  WEAK DATA POINT WAY TO FURTHER RATE CUTS \par \par \uc2  Weak economic data out on Monday indicated that there could be further scope for central banks to cut interest rates in an attempt to ward off the global slowdown. \par \par \uc2  On top of the euro zone industrial output figures came further indications of cooling inflation in Germany, the 12-nation bloc's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  September inflation in the eastern state of Brandenburg dropped to 2.8 percent year-on-year from 3.1 percent in August while in North Rhine-Westphalia in the west of the country it dipped to two percent from 2.4. \par \par \uc2  The data followed similarly encouraging data from eastern Saxony on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile in Britain, car output fell to 97,000 in August from 115,000 in July, and figures for the last three months showed a 3.5 percent drop on a year earlier. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010924dx9o00mdq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
