\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks stage tentative recovery. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Wheatley, Asian Economics Correspondent \par \uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2001\par 09:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Asian stock markets forged higher on Monday as adventurous investors sought out long-term bargains among blue-chip shares that have fallen hard since deadly attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  But brokers said the sheer uncertainty of the expected U.S. military response to the strikes meant it was premature to declare that markets were finding a bottom, especially as the global economic bill for the carnage was still being drawn up. \par \par \uc2  "Bargain hunting is only for the very, very brave," said Lee Heng Guie, chief economist at HLG Capital in Kuala Lumpur. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, European shares were also expected to open on a relatively stable footing on Monday after losing nearly 20 percent since the September 11 attacks on the World Trade Center in New York and the Pentagon in Washington. \par \par \uc2  The risk of conflict took its toll on Asian dollar bonds on Monday but continued to buoy the Swiss franc, a traditional safe haven in times of trouble. \par \par \uc2  The yen was also strong, despite the threat of a repeat of last week's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent an abrupt strengthening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further depressing Japan's already limp economy. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Seoul, up 2.09 percent, and in Hong Kong, up 1.86 percent, spearheaded Asia's tentative advance. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets in Japan were closed on Monday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  "There's a lack of confidence in the market now...but prices have fallen a lot lately so some investors may think it's a good time to buy," said Frederick Tsang, research director at China Everbright Securities in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Banking heavyweight HSBC Holdings and mainland cellular operator China Mobile led the recovery. \par \par \uc2  But after last week's 14.26 percent drop in the Dow Jones Industrial Average, the second-biggest weekly drop since the summer of 1933 in the depths of the Great Depression, several analysts were unconvinced that the worst was over for Asia. \par \par \uc2  "It's nothing more than a technical rebound. The outlook for the economy remains gloomy and if you're a long-term investor, you don't have to rush into the market now," said Eric Wong, market strategist at Dao Heng Securities in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  SHADOW OF WAR \par \par \uc2  Shares in Bombay, Singapore and Thailand also nosed higher, but bourses in Taiwan and the Philippines beat a retreat. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody is talking of a war but you don't know what's really going to happen. It's the long wait...and the market is getting jittery, you are seeing support levels broken," said Enrique Santa Ana of DBS-Vickers Securities in Manila. \par \par \uc2  The story was the same in the debt markets, where risk-averse investors sold lower-quality paper and switched in to higher-rated corporate and sovereign bonds. \par \par \uc2  "There's a little bit of panic selling in early Asia," said a senior bond dealer with a European investment bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The JP Morgan Emerging Market Bond Index Plus, a widely followed measure of bond investors' willingness to incur risk, widened 17 basis points to 996 bps. \par \par \uc2  This means investors were demanding an interest rate premium of nearly 10 percentage points to compensate them for the risk of investing in developing country bonds instead of U.S. Treasury bonds. \par \par \uc2  A general flight from risk also underpinned the Japanese yen and the Swiss franc at the expense of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan bought as much as $10 billion on Friday, according to dealers' estimates, to cap a damaging surge in the yen that is being fuelled by the repatriation of funds by Japanese banks and companies. \par \par \uc2  The fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  persisted, despite Tokyo's holiday closure, but the yen still nosed ahead to 116.35 per dollar from 116.61 at Friday's close in New York. \par \par \uc2  The yen and the Swiss franc are also drawing strength from the unwinding of so-called carry trades, when investors borrow low-interest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in risker, higher-yielding assets. To repay these borrowings, investors are buying yen and, in particular, the Swiss franc. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , additionally buoyed by safe-haven flows, gave up some ground on Monday to 1.4472 per euro from an all-time high on Friday of 1.4396. \par \par \uc2  Market risks soared at the weekend as a second wave of U.S. war planes, ships and troops moved to within striking distance of Afghanistan, which is believed to be harbouring Osama bin Laden, suspected by Washington of masterminding the September 11 attacks that killed nearly 7,000 people. \par \par \uc2  The shadow of war might have been expected to send oil prices soaring. But the shadow of global recession is, for now, even longer, and oil prices continued to fall after heavy losses last week. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark November NYMEX crude futures ended a shortened after-hours session at $25.65 a barrel, down 32 cents from Friday, when it lost 76 cents or nearly three percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010924dx9o00fab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
