\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen and franc still favoured by the risk averse. \b0\par\par\uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2001\par 06:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The yen and Swiss franc kept their strong tone on Monday with risk aversion and repatriation set to be the dominant themes for a third week running. \par \par \uc2  But while the dollar has lost ground to both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last week's large scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and an exodus out of long euro positions has helped limit the pain. \par \par \uc2  Traders were on alert for a repeat performance from the BOJ on Monday even though Japanese markets are shut for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been know to engage in sneak \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before and dealers do not doubt its determination given it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on no less than three days last week. It also took the unusual step on Friday of buying dollars during New York trading hours, albeit only through Japanese banks in Tokyo. Since the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian hours as well on Friday, traders suspected the total amount purchased could be upwards of $10 billion - making it more than that bought during Monday and Wednesday combined. \par \par \uc2  Yen bulls are also keeping a wary eye on Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's visit to the United States this week, just in case \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are mentioned when he meets President George W. Bush on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers, however, doubt something as parochial as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate policy would be a major topic for discussion given the momentous events of recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  For their part, Japanese exporters and institutions still have yen buy orders from 117.00 upwards for hedging and repatriation reasons, keeping the dollar's bias to the downside. \par \par \uc2  In afternoon trading on Monday the dollar was pinned at 116.45 yen having finished Friday at 116.61 in New York. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also subdued at 106.40 yen after slumping from atop 108.00 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Part of the rout was attributed to unwinding of yen carry trades, where investors borrowed at low interest rates in yen to lend at higher rates in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the euro. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc had been another popular low-cost funding vehicle, which was one reason it leapt to record highs on the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday around 1.4396. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the franc was sitting at 1.4450 per euro having rallied almost five percent since September 11's devastating attacks on Washington and New York. It was at 1.5825 to the dollar against 1.5789 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The flight from euro/Swiss and euro/yen positions was of such size that it dragged the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down on the dollar, even though the market's first instinct had been to see the attacks as a negative for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Instead the euro was languishing at $0.9136 on Monday after falling over a cent on Friday from $0.9275. \par \par \uc2  Bank of France Governor Jean-Claude Trichet tried to soothe market worries, saying on Sunday that last week's record declines on European share markets are "clearly" exagerrated and that economic growth in the region is likely to accelerate next year. \par \par \uc2  Trichet's words echoed those of other European finance chiefs, who have said they expect the region's economies to remain strong thanks to falling inflation and cuts in taxes and interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Private sector economists are far less optimistic. \par \par \uc2  An expert at Germany's Halle Institute for Economics (IWH), for instance, was quoted as saying on Sunday that the attacks on the U.S. could cost 100,000 jobs in Europe's largest economy. \par \par \uc2  Economist Udo Ludwig said while U.S. consumers had already been holding back before the September 11 attacks, they would be even more likely to do so now, which would hurt Germany's export-dependent auto manufacturers in particular. \par \par \uc2  Just how cautious consumers had become should be illustrated by the Conference Board confidence survey due on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters survey of economists produced an average forecast of 105.1 in September a steep drop from 114.3 the month before. However, with little to compare the shock to, any estimate is likely to be a rough one and some go as low as 90.0. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0437 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Last     US Close  %Chg   YTD %  2000 Cls\par                               21 Sep.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro/dlr             0.9136   0.9147   -0.12   -3.79   0.9496\par Dlr/yen              116.40   116.57   -0.15   +1.70   114.45\par Euro/yen             106.40   106.55   -0.14   -2.12   108.71\par Dlr/swiss            1.5824   1.5789   +0.22   -1.17   1.6012\par Stg/dlr              1.4570   1.4567   +0.02   -2.87   1.5000\par Dlr/can              1.5708   1.5710   -0.01   +5.32   1.4914\par Aus/dlr              0.4870   0.4842   +0.58  -12.94   0.5594\par Euro/swiss           1.4433   1.4433   +0.00   -5.18   1.5221\par Eu