\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Asia stocks mostly weaker but Hong Kong up. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Valerie Lee \par \uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2001\par 05:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 24 (Reuters) - Most Asian bourses sagged in early trade on Monday following Wall Street's worst week since the Great Depression as markets steeled themselves for U.S. retaliation for the deadly attacks on New York and Washington. \par \par \uc2  But shares in Hong Kong and Seoul edged higher as investors sought out bargains among shares that have fallen heavily since the September 11 attacks raised the spectre of global recession. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Tokyo were closed for the Autumnal Equinox day holiday and will resume trading on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Oil markets saw U.S. crude futures skidding further in off-hour ACCESS trading after heavy losses last week while the dollar softened in holiday-thinned trade. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark November NYMEX crude futures ended a shortened session at $25.65 a barrel, down 32 cents from Friday, when it lost 76 cents or nearly three percent. \par \par \uc2  "This is a classic global market panic," John Schofield, a technical analyst at Prudential-Bache Securities in Hong Kong, said over the weekend. "I think we are in the final stage of it but whether that will last another week or a few days I don't know." \par \par \uc2  With investors' appetite for risk shrinking, capital is expected to keep flowing into cash, short-term bonds, gold and the Swiss franc, which rose sharply last week and held on to its gain in early Monday trading. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buoyed by safe-haven flows and the unwinding of carry trades, held firm around 1.5800 per dollar and a record high of 1.4450 per euro \par \par \uc2  The yen remained in demand but the threat of renewed Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented it from pushing beyond 116.50 per dollar. The yen rose smartly on Friday to close at 116.61 per dollar in New York despite dollar purchases by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that dealers said may have reached $10 billion. \par \par \uc2  POISED TO SELL \par \par \uc2  In the equity markets many investors had their fingers poised on the sell button, and bourses in Europe are expected to extend last week's falls. \par \par \uc2  However, signs of stabilisation in Hong Kong and Seoul encouraged speculation that U.S. stocks might see a bounce as long-term investors snap up drastically discounted stocks. \par \par \uc2  The Dow closed 1.68 percent lower on Friday at 8,235.81 after initially dropping more than three percent. The broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index sank 1.9 percent, while the tech-laced Nasdaq Composite Index tumbled 3.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Dow was down 14.26 percent for the week, second only to a 15.5 percent drop in the summer of 1933, according to the research firm MarketHistory.com. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index of 33 blue chip stocks was up 1.21 percent in late morning with brokers reporting buying in oversold stocks including banking giant HSBC Holdings \par \par \uc2  Shares in HSBC have been hammered recently as investors worry that its global exposure makes it especially vulnerable if the global economy tips into recession as some economists fear. \par \par \uc2  "What we are staring into now is a very negative scenario for weaker growth, deeper gloom on the economic front and more bad news before we can see the glimmers of light," said David Brown, chief European economist with Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's benchmark TAIEX index was down 3.40 percent, hurt by the U.S. performance and heavy margin calls, while Singapore's Straits Times index was down 1.34 percent and the Australian benchmark index down 1.1.18 percent. \par \par \uc2  SPOT GOLD UP \par \par \uc2  Gold opened higher in Asia on the dollar's weakness against the Japanese yen but then slipped back. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold opened at US$292.25/3.25 an ounce in Hong Kong but then fell to $290.75/1.75, below New York's close on Friday of US$291.20/2.20. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still nervous and volatility is high as you can see from the wide spread," said a trader with a market maker in Sydney. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010924dx9o007kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
