\par\par \b \uc2  Asian Stocks Slide Amid Lingering Pessimism; Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2 1,461 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 06:25\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  Major Asian-Pacific stocks slid Friday, dragged down by lingering pessimism about the global economy in the wake of last week's terrorist attacks in the U.S. The dollar rose against the yen in Asia after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  staged its third \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares fell more than 2% ahead of a long Japanese holiday weekend, but rallied after tumbling to an 18-year low. The benchmark Nikkei 225 average lost 230.17 points, or 2.4%, to close at 9554.99. At one point in morning trading, the Nikkei index fell as low as 9382.95 - its lowest level since early December 1983. Thursday, the average slid 154.44 points, or 1.6%. \par \par \uc2  Friday was the last day for transaction settlements before the end of the fiscal first half on Sept. 30, but many traders retreated to the sidelines in the afternoon session ahead of the three-day weekend. Financial markets in Japan will be closed Monday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei index fell in line with Thursday's decline on Wall Street as major U.S. companies announced layoffs and profit warnings linked to the attacks on the World Trade Center in New York and the Pentagon in Washington. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty weighted heavy on Wall Street, where investors and analysts wondered just how much and for how long the economy will suffer as a result of the Sept. 11 attacks, and anxiously awaited to see how the Bush administration will retaliate. \par \par \uc2  Thursday in New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average skidded 382.85 points, or 4.4%, to close at 8376.28. The Nasdaq Composite Index fell 56.87, or 3.7%, to 1470.93. \par \par \uc2  Massive layoffs in U.S. industry - particularly the aviation business, which was hit hard after terrorists hijacked passenger jets and crashed them into New York's World Trade Center and the Pentagon in Washington - could snowball into economic calamity, some experts fear. \par \par \uc2  Thursday, the three biggest European markets - in London, Frankfurt and Paris - fell sharply after rallying briefly at the start, with British stocks lurching to a four-year low.  "At the moment, the 'fear factor' is what's driving the market downward," said David Thwaites, European stock strategist at BNP Paribas. \par \par \uc2  "It's a vicious cycle we are seeing here," said Nigel Scott, a head of institutional equities at ABN Amro in New Zealand. "Investors are beginning to count the costs to businesses and with information about job losses going around in the U.S., the impact seeps down to consumer spending," Mr. Scott said. \par \par \uc2  War fears have filled world markets with jitters, with President Bush telling his nation that Washington is demanding that Afghanistan's ruling Taliban hand over terrorism suspects, including alleged mastermind Osama bin Laden, and shut terrorist-training camps in the impoverished nation. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar slid to a seven-month low against the yen in overseas markets Thursday, and initially continued its decline in early Tokyo trading. But the dollar rebounded after Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. Japanese Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Haruhiko Kuroda confirmed that financial authorities - concerned that the yen's strength is putting the country's already weak economy under further strain - had bought dollars and sold yen to try to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the third this week by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and traders said the action was widely anticipated. The dollar has been hit by continuing skepticism about whether Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be sufficient to cap the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday in Asia, the dollar was quoted at 117.15 yen, above 116.29 yen late Thursday in New York. The dollar sank as low as 115.82 yen in New York trading, its lowest level since Feb. 26. Friday in Asia, it rose as high as 117.37 yen. The euro was at 92.07 cents, lower than 92.67 cents late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting other volatile Asian markets: \par \par \uc2  - In Hong Kong, shares plunged below the 9000 barrier for the first time since October 1998, as investors fled from equities for cash. The blue-chip Hang Seng Index ended down 383.78 points, or 4.1%, at 8934.20. It traded the session in a range between 8894.36 and 9039.65. The broader All Ordinaries Index finished down 145.12 points at 3865.98. \par \par \uc2  Heavy selling in HSBC Holdings and other blue-chip heavyweights led the decline as anticipation of imminent U.S. military action intensified. \par \par \uc2  - In Seoul, South Korean stocks closed lower on concerns about possible massive damage to high-tech companies that are a backbone to South Korea's economy. The Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, shed 1.7%, or 7.96 points, to end at 472.31, its high for the day. \par \par \uc2  "People are worried about what's going to happen in the U.S. particularly with tech stocks," said Abdul Wahid Butt, head of research at Indosuez W.I. Carr Securities in Seoul. "There is no point in holding them." \par \par \uc2  - In Taipei, Taiwanese shares ended at an 8 1/2-year low on continued selling by foreign investors. The Weighted Price Index of the Taiwan Stock Exchange closed 106.99 points, or 2.9%, lower at 3591.85. It was the bourse's lowest close since 3578.10 recorded Feb. 10, 1993. \par \par \uc2  Foreign institutional investors sold a net six billion New Taiwan dollars (US$173.6 million) worth of Taiwan shares over the past two days, compounding worries over capital outflow from Taiwan in the wake of attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's tech investors tend to take their cue from the tech-heavy Nasdaq because of the close ties between local manufacturers and electronics companies in the U.S. Taiwan is a leading producer of the world's chips, computers, and peripherals. \par \par \uc2  - In Manila, Philippine shares closed at a three-year low, in step with Wall Street's losses Thursday, as investors continued to unload their holdings in view of the deteriorating economic outlook both for the country and the U.S., traders said. The 30-company Philippine Stock Exchange Index ended down 14.58 points, or 1.2%, at 1162.85, its lowest closing level since Sept. 23, 1998, when it ended at 1159.69. The index declined 0.8% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Friday's loss brings the total decline of the index to 3.6% since Wednesday. "We can blame Wall Street, we can blame uncertainties on what would happen now with U.S. fight against terrorism," said AB Capital Securities analyst Jose Vistan. \par \par \uc2  Prospects of a weaker U.S. economy and its adverse effect on the Philippines, which regards the U.S. as its most important trading partner, have also driven investors to the sidelines, traders said. \par \par \uc2  - New Zealand shares finished in negative territory but cut their losses toward the close, as a few defensive stocks and Telecom Corp. provided some positive impetus after Wall Street's decline. However, brokers say trading continued to be tentative as businesses around the globe come to terms with the fallout from last week's terrorist attacks in the U.S., brokers say. By the finish, the NZSE-40 Capital index was down 11.62 points, or 0.6%, at 1806.57, but off its intraday low of 1779.07. \par \par \uc2  - In Sydney, the Australian share market closed lower. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index fell 41.4 points, or 1.4%, to 2941.9 points, while the ASX All Ordinaries index shed 40.4 points, or 1.4%, at 2884.5 points. \par \par \uc2  - In Shanghai, China's domestic shares finished lower, as the hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares crumbled with the weak U.S. and regional markets, traders said. The Shanghai B-share index dropped 2.5% to 146.89, while the Shenzhen B-share index fell 4.8% to 229.24. The bearishness spilled over to the local investors-only A-share markets, with the Shanghai A-share index dipping 0.6% to close at 1890.41, and the Shenzhen A-share index declining 0.8% to 557.09. \par \par \uc2  B shares are denominated in U.S. and Hong Kong dollars and open to both foreign and domestic investors, while A shares are denominated in yuan and open only to locals. \par \par \uc2  - Indian shares fell to a sharply lower close on worries about armed conflict between the U.S. and Afghanistan. The Bombay Sensex Index tumbled 5.8%. \par \par \uc2  - Pakistan's market remained closed by authorities for the fifth straight day. \par \par \uc2  - This report was compiled from the Associated Press and Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020010921dx9l00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
