\par\par \b \uc2  Hayami: Will Meet Market Fund Demand As Needed \b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 07:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- It is natural for the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets' view of the U.S. economy and the dollar, which have been strong until now, to shift following recent developments, Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. economy and the dollar have been strong (until now) but its a natural development that there are shifts to how they are viewed," Hayami said, referring to the dollar's sharp decline against the yen following last week's terrorists attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  "We will make efforts to react flexibly to developments," in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Hayami said in parliament. \par \par \uc2  Hayami said the BOJ reacted promptly to last week's terrorists attacks by setting up a task force to ensure stability in the financial system, and by providing increased liquidity to the market. \par \par \uc2  "We kept in touch with central banks overseas ... and did everything we could do to make sure that enough liquidity was provided," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ governor added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to ease monetary policy further at its board meeting on Tuesday, in order to bring about stronger easing effects. \par \par \uc2  "We acted out of the need to cooperate internationally," Hayami said about the BOJ's decision to ease after the U.S. Federal Reserve Board and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates the previous day. \par \par \uc2  "We intend to continue to keep in contact with overseas central banks," to provide ample liquidity to ensure smooth settlements and to stabilize the financial sector. \par \par \uc2  "We will watch with the utmost care and act flexibly," Hayami said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-00G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0600GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-01G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0601GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-01G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0601GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-02G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0602GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-02G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0602GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 21/09/01 06-04G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0604GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have\par been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets intermittently this week to\par support the dollar and will continue to do so for the time being.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We will likely continue taking such action for the time being, while\par watching U.S. economic and political developments, " Hayami said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the decision of whether or not to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is up the finance\par ministry, and not the BOJ, Hayami's comments indicate that Tokyo will\par continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to keep the dollar supported.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, the MOF and the BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the third time this\par week, pushing the dollar up to Y117.37 from around Y116.32.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami declined to say, however, what would be an appropriate\par dollar/yen level for the Japanese economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates are decided by the market ... and I won't\par comment on specific levels or numbers," Hayami said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Currently, "there is uncertainty surrounding the dollar after last\par week's coordinated terrorist attacks in the U.S., which is making the\par yen stronger," Hayami said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates ought to reflect the state of Japan's\par economy, Hayami said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami added, however, that if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates fluctuate so\par largely so that they hamper \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trade with other countries,\par "appropriate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the ones the finance ministry has been\par saying that they have taken and will take, are needed."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami also said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been leaving the excess yen\par generated from dollar-buying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the money market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has up until now "sterilized" its\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or absorbed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -generated yen from the market\par through its daily money market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , has refrained from doing so\par after Monday and Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par are seen as a form of monetary easing, and are said to help weaken the\par yen.\par   -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par miyako.takebe@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hayami said the BOJ will continue to provide liquidity in the\par aftermath of financial market uncertainty following the attacks in the\par U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, he added he didn't think there would be much financial market\par disruption or a shortage of funds ahead of Japan's fiscal first-half at\par the end of September.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Though the outlook for the U.S. and the world economy remain\par uncertain, we took steps at our policy board meeting (Tuesday) to ensure\par that we can continue to provide as much liquidity becomes necessary," to\par meet banks' demands for funds, Hayami said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "For the time being, I think we can make do with these steps," Hayami\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010921dx9l00088\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
