\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Watch: Confidence In Hayami Hit As Governor Fumbles \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Shindo \par \uc2 1,109 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 04:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami has his fair\par share of worries - severe deflation, a creaky banking system and a\par shrinking economy. But recently he appears to have given himself another\par problem: convincing financial markets he's still up to the job.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  In the last month or so, market participants have become increasingly irritated at Hayami's handling of monetary policy. He has repeatedly said one thing and done another, leading some to question his very ability to lead. \par \par \uc2  This all comes at a bad time for Japan. \par \par \uc2  The world's second-largest economy is almost surely falling into its fourth recession in a decade, and the outlook for the world economy has darkened in the wake of last week's terrorist destruction in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's decision in March to slam interest rates back to zero and flood the financial system with liquidity has proved ineffective in taming deflation. Three years of falling prices have crimped consumer spending and increased the burden on the nation's army of heavily indebted corporations. \par \par \uc2  It's at like times like this, some observers say, that Japan needs a strong, dependable and inspiring figure running the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Instead, in the last couple of months, the BOJ has acted in ways directly opposite to Hayami's pronouncements. \par \par \uc2  Take this week, for example. \par \par \uc2  Following half-point rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other major central banks on Monday, expectations were running high that the BOJ would ease policy on the first day of a regular two-day policy board meeting scheduled to end Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But Hayami on Tuesday morning - during the BOJ's self-imposed "blackout" against public comments before policy meetings - was quoted by a news agency as saying the BOJ would "not at all" cut the meeting short and ease with the other banks. \par \par \uc2  That evening, the policy board did just that - cutting the official discount rate and raising its target for pumping excess cash into the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Hayami later said the BOJ had decided to ease policy after the Fed and ECB acted. \par \par \uc2  To many, Hayami's misleading remark reflected dithering at a time when decisive, speedy action was needed to shore up battered sentiment. \par \par \uc2  The apparent indecisiveness was all the more remarkable coming amid what otherwise looked like a precisely coordinated 24-hour wave of action by the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the first such demonstration of unity since the mid-1980s Plaza Accord. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, Hayami is so out of touch with global sentiment and with markets, he shouldn't be where he is now," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese brokerage. \par \par \uc2  This wasn't the first time Hayami has confused the markets on monetary policy. Last month's surprise easing also caused doubts about the bank's ability to send the markets a coherent message. \par \par \uc2  In the week before the move, Hayami adamantly insisted a further easing was off the BOJ's radar screen, as it wanted to continue monitoring the effects of previous steps. \par \par \uc2  Few questioned the legitimacy of easing credit again, given Japan's grim and worsening economy, but the governor emerged looking like a king who had lost the support of his subjects, said critics. \par \par \uc2  Many suspect the independent BOJ buckled under fierce pressure from the government, which, sources say, insisted on dispensing with the scheduled second day of BOJ deliberation. \par \par \uc2  In addition to handling the BOJ's appointed duties, Hayami has at times appeared eager to express his views on matters that aren't within his remit. \par \par \uc2  Early this month, asked if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was considering coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other countries to weaken the yen, Hayami, an avowed yen bull, said: "I'm not considering it at all." \par \par \uc2  Hayami's remark gave the yen a boost, but was quickly 'clarified' by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spin-masters sensitive to the fact that the BOJ doesn't set Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  Traders could be forgiven for seizing on Hayami's remark since it is the BOJ that buys or sells yen in the market when Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the bank acts solely as the agent for the Finance Ministry. \par \par \uc2  All Hayami meant, bank spokesmen tried awkwardly to explain to reporters, was that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't up to the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  "I don't know why he just doesn't keep his mouth shut about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said an economist at a foreign bank in Tokyo. "All it does is cause confusion." \par \par \uc2  Criticism of the 76-year old governor isn't new. \par \par \uc2  Back in August 2000, Hayami came under fire for raising interest rates - for the first time in a decade - just as Japan was entering an economic downturn. \par \par \uc2  Many members of the government have yet to forgive Hayami for that mistake, now fueling calls for the bank to take more radical measures to prop up the relapsing economy. \par \par \uc2  A group of disgruntled members of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party are demanding legislative changes to force the BOJ to set inflation targets. \par \par \uc2  Economy Minister Heizo Takenaka, while not one of the BOJ-bashers, has mused aloud about the applicability of the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  model in Japan. There, inflation targets are set after consultation between the governor and the finance minister. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to achieve the targets, the governor can be removed by the government. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, as much as they might be irked at Hayami's actions, few in the Tokyo financial markets are calling for the governor's head. \par \par \uc2  Some say Hayami, who worked as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  staffer for much of his career, is simply a throwback to the era when BOJ governors routinely misled reporters who asked difficult questions. \par \par \uc2  Others feel true sympathy for the man. \par \par \uc2  "There is a loss of confidence in Hayami, but many just feel sorry for him," said strategist Noboru Kawai at Morgan Stanley. "I think he's just really tired out." \par \par \uc2  Indeed, sources say, Hayami was ready to retire in late March and a backdoor deal had been brokered by a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief and a powerful LDP faction on a successor. \par \par \uc2  But the deal came unraveled when the faction's candidate for prime minister, Ryutaro Hashimoto, lost in an upset to Junichiro Koizumi. \par \par \uc2  Hayami would still be happy to step down, sources say. But, adds Kawai: "The problem is that the BOJ doesn't want to let him go. He has no obvious successor." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Nick Shindo, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929;\par nick.shindo@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1042 4131 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010921dx9l0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
