\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr Briefly Above Y116.80; Traders Cite BOJ In Mkt Again \b0\par\par\uc2 406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 15:46\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar briefly spiked above Y116.80 in early New York trade Friday, with traders attributing the move to more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Several traders said they had seen the BOJ in the market, but it was unclear whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted in New York, with some traders saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in London. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York, they never do," said one analyst at a foreign bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ confirmed earlier that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian trade, buying the dollar through a range of banks, and it was also reported to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during London trading. \par \par \uc2  Around 9:30 a.m. EDT (1330 GMT) in New York, the dollar was at Y116.54, off its high of Y116.81 at 9:11 a.m. EDT (1311 GMT). \par \par \uc2  Attempts to confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unsuccessful. Officials couldn't immediately be reached as the bank has moved its offices due to last week's attacks on the World Trade Center. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in New York meanwhile couldn't confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A Federal Reserve spokesman declined to comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation, citing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's routine policy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia and elsewhere has had limited effect on the yen, underscoring the fact that the BOJ, acting alone, can't have an appreciable impact on the dollar/yen pair. \par \par \uc2  "As you can see, it's having no effect," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Investors Bank and Trust in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "I don't understand why they're not coming in with more money to try to prop it up more," he said. \par \par \uc2  Mazanec suggested the BOJ's best bet would be to "buy some time" ahead of the mid-fiscal year close at the end of September, when there will be less need for yen, than try to push back a tidal wave of demand. \par \par \uc2  But the drop-in-the-ocean effects of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  bode ill for future success of Japanese officials' "jawboning" methods, he said, where threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can keep the yen strength tamed. \par \par \uc2  "At this point," he said, "the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at all-time lows." \par \par \uc2  -By Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010921dx9l004bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
