\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ takes lonely fight against strong yen overseas. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Daniel Sternoff \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 21:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in European and U.S. trading hours on Friday appeared to be conducted solely through banks in Tokyo, bolstering analysts' convictions that Japan is isolated in its efforts to curb export-crippling yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan (BOJ) bought dollars and sold yen on the open market for the third day this week on Friday, but for the first time took action during New York trading hours. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. morning, New York traders said they had not seen direct dollar purchases by the BOJ or by other central banks acting on its behalf, as often happens during \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Japanese banks staffed late in Tokyo were making a market for the BOJ," said David Gilmore, partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics in Essex, Connecticut. \par \par \uc2  Alan Ruskin, research director at 4CAST Ltd, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York trading was a warning to traders outside of Tokyo's time zone that it would take its battle to tame yen strength to any market at any time. \par \par \uc2  But by acting through Tokyo banks, "it seems they might want to avoid any sense of confusion that the Fed was a part of it," Ruskin said. \par \par \uc2  Had the BOJ enlisted other central banks to act on its behalf, Gilmore said it could have fueled rampant speculation that the U.S. Treasury or other Group of Seven major industrial nations were adopting a common stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, global central banks acted in rare unison by sharply reducing interest rates and taking steps to pump liquidity into world financial markets in the wake of last week's devastating attacks on New York and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  But with global markets already extremely volatile, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market watchers suggested Japan may not want to help fuel the vivid imaginations of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players. \par \par \uc2  And by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only through banks in its own time zone, it highlighted that G7 nations do not share its fight, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "There is no way the U.S. Treasury would agree to step in and buy dollars at this point," Gilmore said. \par \par \uc2  Japan has repeatedly warned it will not brook a yen so strong as to threaten its export-dependent economy, but neither the United States nor top European nations have signaled \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a top economic priority in the wake of the attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  U.S. DOLLAR BUYING COUNTERPRODUCTIVE TO FED \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists said any U.S. cooperation in propping up the dollar could be counterproductive to the Fed's efforts to pour massive amounts of liquidity into the financial system to keep markets from seizing up. \par \par \uc2  "It would be futile for the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now because the Fed is pumping in liquidity, which depresses the dollar. For the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it effectively means the U.S. is withdrawing liquidity to make the dollar stronger," said Michael Rosenberg, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  "That is not what the U.S. is trying to accomplish. The U.S. just isn't focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now," he said. \par \par \uc2  He suggested that the dollar could regain ground against the yen once financial markets settled down, allowing the Fed to reabsorb the flood of cash it has unleashed into the financial system. \par \par \uc2  "I think dollar/yen has some upside here," Rosenberg said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar is down over 4 percent from around 122 yen since the Sept. 11 attacks, and Japanese authorities began scooping up dollars once the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped below 117 yen. \par \par \uc2  In late Friday trade, the dollar hovered around 116.60 yen , with the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helping prop the dollar up from Thursday's seven-month low near 115.80 yen. \par \par \uc2  But because the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held up well against the euro, losing only around 1.3 percent despite severe declines in U.S. equity markets, analysts said there appeared to be little immediate threat a falling dollar could force overseas investors to flee dollar-based U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar has remained remarkably stable. It could have weakened much more than it has in this environment," Ruskin said. "I think the U.S. only gets concerned if they see the dollar becoming a negative force in other dollar asset markets." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9l00xho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
