\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar seen pushing lower despite BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Isabel Reynolds \par \uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 10:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - Only the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) stands between Japan's ailing exporters and the pain of an even lower dollar, so its efforts to hold down the yen may be no more than a partial success, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But the market focus will eventually turn away from the aftermath of last week's devastating attacks on the United States, and the dollar could recover in the second half of next year, according to some market-watchers. \par \par \uc2  Jitters over the economic and other fallout from last week's events have dragged the greenback from over 121 yen to a trough of around 115.80, despite repeated efforts by the Japanese authorities to prop it up around 117. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ can partially offset speculative sales, but pressure on the dollar will continue," said Satoru Ogasawara, a strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ knows the market has a lot of power, so it won't try to do the impossible," said Takayuki Togawa, a manager at Tokai Bank. "I think it's more interested in smoothing out the market." \par \par \uc2  All agree that Japan - worried that yen strength will further damage its export-dependent economy - is highly likely to remain isolated in its efforts to support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. is traditionally very much opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . As long as the dollar does not nose-dive to 100 yen for example, America will probably leave it up to Japan and Europe," said Mamoru Yamazaki, chief economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Repatriation has been a constant theme in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since last week, with nervous investors anxious to keep funds close to hand. \par \par \uc2  But analysts differ as to how capital flows will affect the dollar. \par \par \uc2  REPATRIATION ALL AROUND \par \par \uc2  Figures released by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) on Thursday showed foreign investors had sold off a total of 580.4 billion yen in Japanese assets between 10-14 September. A source at the MOF interpreted this as a sign of U.S. investors selling off bonds to repatriate funds. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, Japanese investors also sold off 284.1 billion yen in foreign securities. \par \par \uc2  "We take the view that downward pressure on the dollar will continue in the near term...With a crisis like this in the United States, capital is likely to flow to the countries that supply it, like Switzerland and Japan," said Ogasawara of CSFB. \par \par \uc2  "In terms of amount, Japanese repatriation is likely to be more significant," he added. \par \par \uc2  Others reckoned U.S. repatriation might also be a force to be reckoned with. \par \par \uc2  "We don't yet know how much it will cost to repair the damage caused last week, but some of the capital to cover that may be repatriated from abroad," said Yamazaki of Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  CONSUMERS KEY \par \par \uc2  With the prospect of military action looming after President George W. Bush demanded that Afghanistan hand over those suspected of being behind the attacks, analysts looked back to the Gulf War of 1991 for clues as to what might happen next. \par \par \uc2  "War would not necessarily be damaging for the dollar as long as it took place outside the United States," said Yamazaki of Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar did weaken during the Gulf War, but America was on the point of recession at that time. You could say the same now, but Japan's economy was relatively much stronger then. This time around, Japan is in recession," he added. \par \par \uc2  Ogasawara took a bleaker view, saying that U.S. consumer confidence was bound to suffer during the planned campaign. \par \par \uc2  "I think the effect will be more severe this time, which will be a dollar-weakening factor. The Michigan University figures showed consumer confidence was low before the attacks, and we expect figures out next week also to be bad," he said. \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment index, compiled before the attacks and published just after, plunged to 83.6 in September - the lowest since November 1993. Economists had forecast the index to read 90.8. \par \par \uc2  There remained comfort of sorts for Japan's exporters. \par \par \uc2  Ogasawara said interest-rate cuts and fiscal stimulus would help kick-start the U.S. economy in the second half of next year and likely trigger a recovery in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Yamazaki said Japan was in for a lengthy recession, but the U.S. should bounce back relatively quickly from the crisis. \par \par \uc2  "In the near term, the dollar will be pressurised, but I don't see it continuing to fall in the medium to long term," he said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9l00eih\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
