\par\par \b \uc2  TOCOM gold ends mixed after seesawing on yen moves. \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 09:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - Yen-based gold futures closed mixed on Friday after seesawing along with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where the dollar bounced from seven-month lows against the yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said trade was relatively light and dominated by position adjustments ahead of a three-day weekend. The Tokyo market is closed on Monday for a public holiday. \par \par \uc2  Individual investors, who had helped to prop up prices by bargain-hunting for gold futures this week, were waiting to take profits. \par \par \uc2  They expected safe-haven gold to surge on any U.S. retaliation for last week's attacks in New York and Washington, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "They were holding onto long positions, wondering when a war will begin," one brokerage analyst said. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark August 2002 gold closed unchanged at 1,081 yen per gram, having trading between 1,070 and 1,084. Other contracts ranged from two yen down to one yen up. \par \par \uc2  Turnover was estimated at 46,493 lots or 46.5 tonnes on Friday, against 41,203 lots on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Open interest rose to 237,958 lots at the end of Thursday trade from 236,814 lots a day earlier and 210,055 lots a week ago, as more speculative money entered the market. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar was pushed to a high of around 117.40 yen from around 116.50 yen by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tokyo on Friday. \par \par \uc2  It had hit a trough of 115.83 yen overnight, its lowest point since February 28. \par \par \uc2  Silver futures closed lower on Friday, erasing most of their gains made on the previous day as New York's rally fizzled. \par \par \uc2  Benchmark August 2002 silver finished down by the daily price limit of 6.0 yen per 10 grams at 166.4. Remaining months lost 3.9 to 6.0 yen. \par \par \uc2  Platinum futures gained two to 16 yen per gram, with benchmark August ending up six yen at 1,671. \par \par \uc2  Palladium futures lost 21 to 53 yen. Benchmark August settled down 21 yen at 1,700. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9l00c70\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
