\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - CORRECTED-GLOBAL ECONOMY-Brave face can't counter recession risk. \b0\par\par\uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      In SINGAPORE story headlined "GLOBAL ECONOMY-Brave face \par \par \uc2   can't counter recession risk" please read in eighth paragraph \par \par \uc2   ...1,200... instead of ...1,2000...(correcting figure). \par \par \uc2  A corrected repeat follows. \par \par \uc2     By Wayne Cole \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 21 (Reuters) - President George W. Bush on Friday put a brave face on the economic outlook, declaring that while terrorists had attacked a symbol of American prosperity they had not touched its source. \par \par \uc2  "America is successful because of the hard work and creativity and enterprise of our people," said Bush in a speech to a packed congress. "These were the true strengths of our economy before September 11, and they are our strengths today." \par \par \uc2  Standing in the teeth of the gale sweeping the world economy, the U.S. Federal Reserve did its best to reassure. \par \par \uc2  Fed Bank of St. Louis President William Poole expressed confidence in the economy and cautioned against over estimating the impact of the attacks, horrifying thought they were. \par \par \uc2  Poole said the U.S. economy will not "suffer great pain", while also emphasising that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plenty of flexibility to cut rates further even though borrowing costs are already at seven-year lows following the emergency easing on Monday. \par \par \uc2  In this he echoed Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony before lawmakers earlier on Thursday, in which he underlined his own belief that the U.S. economy would bounce back. \par \par \uc2  Amongst market participants the immediate hope was that the show of official and political unity would provide a psychological boost for battered U.S. financial markets, and maybe the consumer as well. \par \par \uc2  There was no doubt one was needed with the Dow Jones Industrials Index ending at a three-year trough Thursday having shed 1,200 points this week. \par \par \uc2  WORDS NOT ENOUGH \par \par \uc2  And yet in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market the Bank of Japan was once more compelled to rescue the U.S. dollar as fears of global recession went unabated. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market to buy dollars for yen persistently, lifting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 117.40 yen from seven-month lows offshore around 115.85. \par \par \uc2  This was the third day this week the bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dealers estimate it has bought at least $10 billion, underlining its determination to restrain the yen. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had acted alone and was ready to do so again. \par \par \uc2  Trade Minister Takeo Hiranuma was blunter saying the yen's rise to near 115.00 to the U.S. dollar posed a severe threat to Japan's exporters. \par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaru Hayami said the bank would deal with the bearish trend in the dollar, though without giving details. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  took steps to ease its already hyper-loose monetary policy, but shied away from truly radical measures that some politicians and analysts have argued for. \par \par \uc2  Hayami seemed no close to bending on Friday, rejecting calls for the bank to adopt inflation targets or for buying corporate bonds and commercial paper. \par \par \uc2  The latest indicators out of Japan showed just how vulnerable its economy had been even before the attacks. \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry said its all-industries index - seen as a supply-side proxy for gross domestic product (GDP) - slumped 1.2 percent in July from the previous month, while the tertiary sector index, which covers the service sector, fell 0.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  The economy is teetering on the brink of its fourth recession in a decade and economists polled by Reuters earlier this week said they expect GDP to post its first full-year contraction since fiscal 1998/99. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were looking ahead anxiously to the release of a key euro zone indicator in the shape of the German Ifo business sentiment survey later on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The report is for August but the Ifo has said that around 25 percent of the responses were collected after the September 11 attacks, leaving analysts wondering whether that would bias the survey lower. \par \par \uc2  GOVERNMENT PARACHUTE \par \par \uc2  Share markets across Asia also buckled on fears Bush's blunt warnings to the Taliban presaged imminent military strikes on Afghanistan, which is, geographically speaking, Asia's back yard. \par \par \uc2  Main indices in Japan, South Korea and Taiwan all off more than two percent on Friday, compounding the punishing loss of shareholder wealth seen since last week's attacks. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's top economic planner said the attack combined with a devastating typhoon would cut the island's gross domestic product (GDP) growth by 0.2 percentage point. \par \par \uc2  "The whole year's economic growth rate will be lowered by 0.2 percentage point," Chen Po-chih, chairman of the cabinet's Council for Economic Planning and Development, told parliament. \par \par \uc2  There was some comfort for the ravaged airline sector when U.S. Congressional leaders and the White House on Friday agreed on a $15 billion bailout plan for the industry. \par \par \uc2  The measure was drawn up to help restore the finances of major airlines reeling from the effects on their business of the hijackings that include costly new security measures and plummeting travel demand. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9l009w1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
