\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Nervous market tugs dollar lower against yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2001\par 12:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates price action, adds Japan MOF comments, Afghan edict, ECB monthly report, fresh quotes, changes byline) \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 20 (Reuters) - The dollar fell by a third of a percent against the yen on Thursday on concern about the U.S. response to last week's attacks on U.S. cities, making only a brief surge higher in a market wary of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped nearly half a yen in European trade on reports the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for the third time this week, but subsided after Japan's Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokowa said the ministry had not instructed the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Thin markets in the aftermath of the air attacks on New York and Washington last week exaggerated any intraday moves, analysts and dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 116.65 and 117.10 this week and we got within that range today, so the markets are going to be nervous," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. \par \par \uc2  "The uncertainty in the market is over the impact of any U.S. response to the attacks." \par \par \uc2  A meeting of Afghanistan's senior Islamic clerics issued an edict on Thursday saying they would declare a jihad, or holy war, if the United States attacks. \par \par \uc2  They also recommended Osama bin Laden, identified by the U.S. as the prime suspect in the attacks, should be persuaded to leave Afghanistan whenever possible. \par \par \uc2  The safe haven Swiss franc benefited from the uncertainty, rising to record highs against the euro for the fifth consecutive day. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at 117.28/33 yen at 1010 GMT, after rallying from the day's lows near 117 on talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc rose to a high of 1.4732 francs against the euro. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR BUFFETED \par \par \uc2  Japan has sold yen for dollars on two days this week in a bid to cap its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-crimping strength and has warned that more action could be in the offing. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said the fundamental outlook for the dollar was poor, with Wednesday's "beige book" report from the Federal Reserve showing the United States' economy was sluggish even before last week's attacks. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will remain under pressure as long as there is uncertainty over how hard U.S. consumer confidence will be hit," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "Japan's forays into the market are giving the dollar some support but won't be enough to stop the dollar falling if the market turns against it." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and Wednesday and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, repeated in Tokyo trading hours on Thursday that more action would be taken if needed. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the dollar's near-term fate was tied to the fortunes of U.S. stocks, which are currently seen as the best indicator of consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock index futures were indicating further losses on Wall Street on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ECB UPBEAT \par \par \uc2  But the euro lost nearly a third a percent against the dollar, pressured by its losses against the Swiss franc and the yen, to trade around $0.9235, a cent below six-month highs set last week. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which cut rates by half a point along with the U.S. Federal Reserve on Monday, said in its monthly report on Thursday it could be expected that euro zone inflation would return to levels below its two percent ceiling "in the not too distant future". \par \par \uc2  "Inflation is the main factor in determining further rate cuts, but the comments in the report were not a huge surprise," said Halpenny. \par \par \uc2  The ECB also said the attacks in the U.S. increased uncertainty about the U.S. global outlook, but said it was confident the euro zone slowdown after the attacks would be short-lived. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010920dx9k00i7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
