\par\par \b \uc2  Asian Shares Climb as Tokyo Jumps 3%; Dollar Strong After BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 06:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  Most major Asian stock markets posted moderate gains Wednesday, while Tokyo shares rose almost 3% after investors cheered the previous day's lower-than-expected fall on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  Investors remained cautious, bracing for possible U.S. military retaliation for last week's terrorist attacks. The Taliban's statement, warning Afghans to prepare for a holy war against the U.S., also kept investors on edge. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar was higher in Asia after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time this week, spurring speculation that Japanese authorities will continue to put a floor under the embattled U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Tuesday in New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 17.30 to close at 8903.40. The Nasdaq composite index finished at 1555.08, down 24.47. Monday - the first day of trading since hijacked jetliners smashed into the World Trade Center and the Pentagon - the Dow lost a record 684.81 points and fell below 9,000 for the first time since December 1998. \par \par \uc2  European stocks finished lower Tuesday but a late rally sparked by a modest rise in U.S. shares cut the losses. \par \par \uc2  Most Asian markets followed Wall Street's lead and settled down after a week of volatile trading following the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks in the U.S. Investors watched for developments in the U.S. effort to draw together an antiterrorism coalition and for Afghanistan's reaction to Washington's demands for extradition of Osama bin Laden, the prime suspect in last week's attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon. \par \par \uc2  Shares in Tokyo advanced, although uncertainty and profit-taking kept gains in check. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment was also helped by an overnight interest-rate cut by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , part of a global effort led by the Federal Reserve to calm jittery investors. Lower interest rates tend to boost stock trading because the cost of borrowing becomes cheaper. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's blue-chip Nikkei 225 average gained 259.72 points, or 2.7%, to close at 9939.60, adding to Tuesday's gain of 175.47 points, or 1.9%. \par \par \uc2  Uncertainty and volatility are going to be the norm for some time in equities markets all over the world, said a managing director at a foreign brokerage in Tokyo, but "the Japanese market has been flat on its back, which people are now recognizing as a buying opportunity." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at 117.72 yen, above 117.23 yen late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at 92.56 cents, lower than 92.79 cents late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy the dollar for yen through the Bank of Japan on Wednesday morning, a ministry official said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market just as the dollar threatened to fall below key support at 117.10 yen. \par \par \uc2  The move marked the second \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. The Finance Ministry has repeatedly said it is ready to take action against excessive dollar-yen moves. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which market participants estimated to be worth several hundred million dollars - roughly the same scale as Monday's action - sent the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flying to a high of 118.12 yen. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday, the Bank of Japan eased credit, joining in a global effort led by the U.S. Federal Reserve Board to try to calm jittery investors. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan lowered its largely symbolic official discount rate - the rate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  charges on loans to commercial banks - to 0.1% from 0.25%. \par \par \uc2  Other central banks that have cut their interest rates this week include the U.S., Great Britain, Canada, Switzerland, Sweden, Denmark, Taiwan, Hong Kong and New Zealand. Lower interest rates tend to boost stock trading because the cost of borrowing becomes cheaper. \par \par \uc2  Highlighting other volatile Asian markets: \par \par \uc2  - In Hong Kong, stocks finished slightly stronger, lifted by mainland telecom giants and conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa. The rebound was in part technical and was driven by bargain hunting, analysts said, adding that confidence is still fragile at this stage. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index rose 250.25 points, or 2.6%, to close at 9558.15. It traded the session in a range of 9560.94 and 9377.56. The broader All Ordinaries Index finished ahead 106.62 points at 4101.60. \par \par \uc2  Mainland telecom company China Mobile (Hong Kong) rose 5.9%, while its rival China Unicom finished 7.9% higher. \par \par \uc2  Adrian Fu, investment manager at Investec Asset Management Asia Ltd. said investors snapped up China-based telecom stocks because they don't have global exposure. \par \par \uc2  - In Seoul, South Korean stocks finished higher, supported by retail investors' buying after the Bank of Korea's interest-rate cut before the market opened. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, rose 1.82 points, or 0.4%, to end at 486.75. The over-the-counter Kosdaq advanced 2%, or 0.98 point, to 50.46. \par \par \uc2  After cutting the one-day call rate by one-half percentage points to 4.00%, Bank of Korea Governor Chon Chol-Hwan said the move was made to ease the shock on the economy and financial markets after last week's attacks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Korea's export-driven economy is highly dependent on the U.S., where analysts predict a further economic slowdown because of the attacks. \par \par \uc2  Although the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions are unprecedented - it was the first half-percentage-point cut and the first intermeeting gathering - there was little reaction in the stock market until retail investors got wind of the moves, players said. \par \par \uc2  Many players had anticipated the government might cut rates before its next meeting on Oct. 11. \par \par \uc2  - In Taipei, Taiwan shares ended little changed, as support-buying by government-linked funds offset the impact of recent losses on Wall Street and damage caused by Typhoon Nari, analysts and traders said. \par \par \uc2  The typhoon battered the island on Monday and Tuesday, causing floods and delaying shipments of goods destined for export, as well as forcing the closure of the island's financial markets. \par \par \uc2  The Weighted Price Index of the Taiwan Stock Exchange closed 6.55 points, or 0.2%, higher at 3781.17. \par \par \uc2  The government estimated that total damage from Typhoon Nari, which caused at least 55 deaths, could be as high as 12 billion Taiwan dollars ($347.1 million) and be another blow to the ailing economy. \par \par \uc2  - In Manila, Philippine shares finished lower as investors, wary of the uncertainty pervading global markets following the attacks, resumed unloading their portfolio of stocks and heading for the sidelines, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The 30-company Philippine Stock Exchange Index broke its 1200-point psychological support to end down 19.33 points, or 1.6%, at 1187.05, after rising 2.4% Tuesday. The index had already fallen 9.1% since last Tuesday's attacks on New York City and Washington, D.C. \par \par \uc2  - In Wellington, New Zealand shares finished mixed. Modest strides early in the day were progressively erased by a weak Australian market and late order flows, as investors continued to readjust some of their portfolios. The market also shrugged off a surprisingly aggressive half-percentage-point cut to the official cash rate by the Reserve Bank of New Zealand, they said. The NZSE-40 Capital index closed near its intraday low, down 1.01 points at 1829.22. However, advancers beat decliners 56 to 39. Volume was relatively modest. \par \par \uc2  - In Sydney, Australian shares closed higher, keeping the benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index above the key psychological 3000-point level, as investors consolidated their positions with defensive stocks. \par \par \uc2  The strength in the market came from demand for industrial stocks, which benefited from switching out of the resource sector, after a strong rally by mining heavyweights in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  The S&P/ASX 200 index closed up 24.8 points, or 0.8%, at 3034.9 points, its high for the day. Its predecessor, the ASX All Ordinaries index, rose 25.6 points, or 0.9%, to 2974.1 points. \par \par \uc2  - In Shanghai, China's stock indexes finished higher on late-session buying in telecommunications and high-technology counters, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  B shares gained 3.6% to end at 152.98 points, while its Shenzhen counterpart rose 3.3% to 247.12. \par \par \uc2  Shanghai's local investors-only A-hare index climbed 1.3% to close at 1905.62. The Shenzhen's A-share index rose 1.3% to 562.99. \par \par \uc2  - This report was compiled from the Associated Press and Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020010919dx9j003ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
