\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:BOJ Sells Yen,Adds  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Funds; Impact Small \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nick Shindo \par \uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 08:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese monetary authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again Wednesday in a move aimed at supporting the dollar, but not thrusting it higher, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The move, which coincided with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to leave the excess yen from its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday in the banks' hands for the first time ever, puts a strong floor under the dollar. But it will hardly make a dot of difference to the selling momentum that has pressured the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the aftermath of the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon last week, participants said. \par \par \uc2  For the second time in three days, the BOJ waded into choppy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell yen, launching the dollar about one yen higher to Y118.10 in midmorning trade. At around 0630 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had settled back to around Y117.90. \par \par \uc2  "We acted alone" without the assistance of other major central banks, Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa told reporters. "I have said we are closely monitoring markets and that we would take appropriate action - as we did today." \par \par \uc2  In typical fashion, Shiokawa refused to reveal the amount of yen offloaded by the BOJ, and he also wouldn't disclose which \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been bought. However, dealers widely reported the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had dumped more than $500 million in yen for dollars - slightly less than the amount the bank was rumored to have sold Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the relatively small amount of yen sold Wednesday suggests Tokyo doesn't have the stomach for an all-out fight with a bearish dollar market and isn't bent on driving the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  upward. But by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice this week near the Y117 level, the finance ministry has made it clear it won't put up with a dollar below that level and, significantly, is likely to enjoy Washington's tacit support for its actions, participants said. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't aimed at pushing up rates, but rather was just to make absolutely clear that Japan won't tolerate the dollar below 117.00," a senior trader at a major Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  "It's a decisive stance," said Toru Umemoto, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it should put a floor under the dollar at Y117.00, or at the very lowest Y115.00." \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010919dx9j000d4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
