\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS - Wall St, Eurostocks tumble, dollar pressured. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Huw Jones \par \uc2 979 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2001\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 19 (Reuters) - More pain for corporate America in the aftermath of last week's attacks, coupled with the threat of U.S. military retaliation, sent Wall Street tumbling with European shares on Wednesday while the dollar was under pressure despite Bank of Japan support for the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Most U.S. government bonds rose, led by interest rate sensitive short-dated issues, as investors sought a safer haven from stocks and the dollar. Crude oil was weak on concern demand would be hit if recession fears come true. \par \par \uc2  The attacks on New York and Washington continued to spur a steady stream of earnings warnings, the latest from photo technology company Eastman Kodak, and aircraft maker Boeing. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts across the world may not be enough to avert a recession, investors feared. \par \par \uc2  "The dilemma for investors is that the Fed stimulus and government resolve makes more certain that we are going to have economic recovery - but it may be further out," said John Davidson, chief investment officer at Circle Trust Co., which has $500 million under management. \par \par \uc2  "There is the immediate negative impact of the loss and the disturbing of consumer confidence." \par \par \uc2  WALL ST, EUROSTOCKS SLIDE \par \par \uc2  U.S. shares tumbled to around three-year lows. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average was down 1.92 percent at 8,732, while the tech-laden Nasdaq Composite shed 2.6 percent to 1,515. \par \par \uc2  The pan-European FTSE Eurotop 300 index was also around three-year lows, off 1.9 percent, having earlier been about a half a percent higher. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear that the attacks will keep U.S. consumers at home, tipping the already teetering economy into recession. \par \par \uc2  U.S. plane maker Boeing said it would lay off between 20,000 and 30,000 workers in its commercial jet unit by the end of 2002 in anticipation of a sharp drop in orders from the troubled airline industry. \par \par \uc2  Eastman Kodak cut its third-quarter profit outlook, blaming softness in its health imaging business and fallout from the attacks, warning that markets across the globe were suffering. \par \par \uc2  "Unfortunately, the U.S. economy is showing signs of further slowing, exacerbated by the September 11 attack," Kodak Chief Financial Officer Bob Brust said. "We are also beginning to see weakening economic conditions in other countries around the world." \par \par \uc2  In Europe, energy stocks tumbled as crude oil prices retreated. TotalFinaElf and BP each lost between 4 and 5 percent after recession fears pushed crude oil prices down sharply on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  OIL PRICES WEAKEN \par \par \uc2  Oil prices eased again after more soothing comments from OPEC on supply security and amid economic worries. \par \par \uc2  London Brent blend futures dipped 43 cents to $26.84 a barrel after dropping more than a dollar on Tuesday. U.S. light crude fell 50 cents to $27.20. \par \par \uc2  Brent, having spiked briefly to $30, is back below the levels seen before last week's attacks on the United States. \par \par \uc2  Gold, another safe haven in times of trouble, fell five cents at its London fixing to $289.35. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Asia, Tokyo's Nikkei blue-chip average closed 2.68 percent higher at 9,939.60 but failed to keep a toehold above the psychologically important 10,000 mark, regained during the morning for the first time since Friday. \par \par \uc2  Central banks unleashed an unprecedented round of intra-meeting interest rate cuts on Monday and Tuesday in an effort to allay fears of a U.S.-led global recession. \par \par \uc2  LONG BONDS SUFFER AS BORROWING SEEN RISING \par \par \uc2  Ultra-long European government bonds yields rose, in line with 30-year U.S. Treasuries, which sold off on Tuesday on fears of a flood of government debt after last week's attacks. Long bond prices pared most losses though as stocks fell. \par \par \uc2  "The 30-year euro debt is suffering on the long T-Bond as there are concerns about all the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cuts we saw this week and awaiting U.S. President (George W.) Bush's $40 billion fiscal loosening package," said a trader in Berlin. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the two-year Schatz was down 1.2 basis points at 3.503 percent, pushing away from two-year lows struck earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  The 30-year Bund yield was up 3.3 basis points at 5.665 percent. \par \par \uc2  Ultra-long dated U.S. Treasury yields steadied at 5.55 percent as U.S. stocks extended losses in morning trade. \par \par \uc2  The dollar pulled itself off recent seven-month lows against the yen after Japan stepped in to buy the shell-shocked \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time this week. But dealers said the greenback would struggle to make headway given fears of possible armed conflict. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment is still against the dollar because of the events in the United States last week and the weak U.S. economic outlook," Halifax treasury economist Nick Mirtchev said. \par \par \uc2  "But Japan seems keen to defend the 117 yen level and we expect more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come if the dollar goes near that level." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suffering a double blow to its economy of falling stock markets and export-crimping yen strength, stepped in to buy dollars in Tokyo for the second time this week. Traders estimated the authorities bought up to $4 billion on Monday in their first round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since April 2000. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose to session highs above 118 yen from 117.13 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but soon faded to 117.26 after defiant comments from Afghanistan's ruling Taliban fueled concern of a prolonged military conflict and deteriorating relations between the Arab and Western world. \par \par \uc2  The euro firmed against the dollar to $0.9300 as stocks fell but was still below six-month highs above $0.9330 set earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  - Additional reporting by James Saft, Natsuko Waki, Richard Mably, George Matlock and Gideon Long. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011008dx9j00wio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
